-- Three School programs, one for each of the founder's books (Shubham Aggarwal), written in the
-- School teaching voice from the books' own text. Every lesson names the chapter it comes from;
-- anything a lesson adds that the book does not say is labelled as the app's own.
-- Generated by scripts/books/build-courses.mjs from scripts/books/courses/*.md. Idempotent: safe to re-run.

-- The Relationship Method
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO programs (id, name, description, price_points, price_cents, currency, delivery_mode, published, outcomes, prerequisites, created_by, created_at) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Relationship Method', 'A course from Shubham Aggarwal''s book The Relationship Method: Clearing Your Emotional Backlog to Unlock Life''s Natural Abundance. Twenty-eight lessons, one chapter at a time: the bucket, energy in storage, the seven relationships pyramid, the flush, and the Daily Emptying Ritual. Every lesson says which chapter it comes from; the pro tips and anything marked as a note from the app are the app''s own.', 0, 0, 'INR', 'online', 1, 'You can name which of your buckets is full, leaky or closed, put each kind of weight in the right relationship, run the four-step flush, and keep the Daily Emptying Ritual every day.', 'None. The book helps but is not needed. Lessons that touch grief or old pain say so first.', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l01', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Sustainment Gap', 'lesson', NULL, 'Day one of a new plan feels great. You feel light. By day fourteen it takes pure willpower, and then it stops.

The book says this is not a flaw in you. It has a name.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, The Invisible Weight.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the Sustainment Gap is.
- Why you cannot out-motivate weight.
- The one question the book wants you to ask instead.

## The gap between starting and lasting

The book calls it the Sustainment Gap: "The space between your ability to start something, and your ability to sustain it."

You know what to do. You read the books. You have the plan. But you cannot keep it up, so you blame yourself.

The book''s answer is plain. Your problem is not a lack of discipline. Your system is overloaded. You are carrying an invisible weight, and "you cannot out-motivate weight."

## Storage full

The book uses a phone. If the screen said "Storage Full," you would not call the phone lazy. You would clear space.

- **A failed habit is a signal:** it is your system flashing a red light.
- **More effort does not close the gap:** the gap is how far you can run with the backpack on.
- **Only dropping weight closes it:** you bridge the gap by putting the backpack down.

## A new question

Stop asking "How do I get more motivated?" The book asks you to look at what is inside you that is not leaving.

> **Pro tip from the app:** the next time a plan breaks on day three, write one line: "Storage full." Then ask what you are carrying, not what is wrong with you.

> **Result:** you stop reading a broken streak as proof that you are weak, and start reading it as a sign of load.

## The bottom line

You are not broken. You are blocked. The gap closes when weight leaves, not when you push harder.', 1, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l02', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The bucket in one picture', 'lesson', NULL, 'Think of everything you feel as water. It pours in all day. The book asks one question: does it ever pour out?

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, The Invisible Weight.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The bucket picture the whole book is built on.
- Why clean water poured into a full bucket only spills.
- Why the book says you run on twenty percent.

## The bucket

When you are born, the book says, the bucket is empty. Energy comes in and energy leaves. Then life happens. A drop of anger. A cup of unsaid words. A gallon of grief you never let out.

You keep pouring in. You never open the valve at the bottom. The water goes still and heavy. That is your emotional backlog.

## Why the new habit spills

You decide to think more positively. In the book''s words, you take "a cup of clean, pure water" and pour it into a full bucket of dirty water. It spills. It makes a mess. The clean water does not clean the bucket.

## The cost of the lid

Keeping all that water pushed down takes energy. The book puts it at about eighty percent of your day. That leaves twenty percent to actually live.

- **Tired for no reason:** you are spending energy on holding the lid down.
- **Small things feel huge:** the bucket is already at the brim.
- **New tactics fail:** there is no room for them.

> **Pro tip from the app:** before you add anything new to your life this week, ask what you could let out first.

> **Result:** you have one simple picture to check your state: how full is the bucket right now?

## The bottom line

Adding to a full bucket only spills. The book''s path is to empty first, then let life pour in.', 2, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l03', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Stuck or blocked', 'lesson', NULL, '"I''m stuck" may be the most common thing people say about their life. The book says it is the wrong word.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Stuck vs. Blocked.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The difference between stuck and blocked.
- How the book reads anxiety.
- What changes when you stop holding the rock.

## Two different problems

Stuck, the book says, means you lack power. A car out of gas on the side of the road. Blocked means something is in the way. The water still wants to flow. The pressure is there. A rock sits in the pipe.

If you think you are stuck, you go looking for more fuel. If you know you are blocked, you look for the rock.

## What anxiety is, in the book''s view

The book calls anxiety "the hum of a pressurized pipe." Energy is trying to move and hits a wall. Pushing harder only adds pressure.

## The story of David

The book tells of a man, David, who lost his drive after a loss he never grieved. He thought he needed a new business or a new marriage. He did not. When the grief was let out, the fire came back by itself.

- **You are not an anxious person:** the book says you may be a healthy person carrying a block.
- **You are not lazy:** an overloaded system shuts down to protect itself.
- **The rock is not you:** a river does not think it is the rock.

> **Pro tip from the app:** swap "I am stuck" for "Something is in the way." Then name one thing that might be in the way.

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app, not the book. If a loss or old pain feels too big to look at alone, talk to a professional or someone you trust.

> **Result:** you have a kinder and more useful way to describe a hard season.

## The bottom line

Stuck asks for more force. Blocked asks you to clear the path. The book says you are blocked.', 3, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l04', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The three lies of fixing', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most self-help treats you like a machine. Find the broken part. Replace it. The book says you are not a machine.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, The Myth of Fixing Yourself.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three lies the book names.
- Why forced positivity can make the weight heavier.
- The book''s line: removal is the great secret.

## You are a river, not a car

You cannot fix a river with a wrench, the book says. If a river stops, the water is not broken. There is a rock in the way.

## The three lies

- **The Lie of Replacement:** you can swap a "bad" feeling for a "good" one. You feel anxious, so you tell yourself to feel grateful. The book calls forced positivity "emotional suppression dressed up as healing."
- **The Lie of Routine:** a better routine will fix you. But a routine is a set of pipes. Pipes do not help if the water is dammed. You need your energy back, not a new schedule.
- **The Lie of Optimization:** track and tune everything. The book tells of Sarah, who tracked sleep, heart rate and even gratitude in a spreadsheet. Her tracking was a way to feel safe. When she let the old fear out, she no longer needed most of it.

## Flushing, not fixing

"Fixing is about changing who you are. Flushing is about removing what you are not." The book''s picture is a sculptor. The statue was already in the marble. Removal shows it.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick one thing you do to "fix" yourself. Ask honestly: is it helping the water move, or helping you hold the lid down?

> **Result:** you can tell a practice that empties you from one that only adds more to carry.

## The bottom line

You do not need more parts. You need less in the way. "Removal is the great secret of natural abundance."', 4, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l05', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The full bucket', 'lesson', NULL, 'You snap at someone over a plate in the sink. Then you promise yourself to be calmer next time. The book says you are missing the whole picture.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, The Bucket Model: The Full Bucket.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The myth of the trigger.
- Why love can feel like pressure when you are full.
- What the book means by "stop trying to carry it better."

## The myth of the trigger

Traffic. A plate. A late text. The book says you did not react to the plate. You reacted to the backlog. The plate was just the drop that broke the surface.

## The signs of a full bucket

- **Tired on waking:** you slept eight hours and you are still heavy.
- **Big reactions to small things:** the bucket is at the brim.
- **Positivity does not land:** you cannot "overwrite trauma with a smile," as the book puts it.
- **Love feels like pressure:** someone pours care into you and there is no room for it. A partner who offers only crumbs can feel safer.
- **Numb at the brim:** to feel anything, you chase extremes.

## Put the bucket down

The book does not want you to handle stress better. It wants you to see that the stress should not be there at all. "Stop trying to carry it better." Put the bucket down and let the water out.

> **Pro tip from the app:** next time you overreact, skip the apology to yourself for "being too much." Ask: what was already in the bucket before this?

> **Careful:** a note from the app. If you feel full for weeks and nothing helps, please talk to a doctor or someone you trust.

> **Result:** you can tell the trigger from the load, and stop fighting the wrong one.

## The bottom line

A full bucket overflows at small things. The fix is not better carrying. It is less to carry.', 5, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l06', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The leaky bucket', 'lesson', NULL, 'You get the praise, the job or the "I love you." For an hour you feel whole. Then it drains away and you need more.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, The Bucket Model: The Leaky Bucket.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What a leaky bucket is.
- Why confidence that comes from outside never lasts.
- The book''s answer: stop pouring.

## Running to the well

The book''s picture: you run to the well all day with tiny cups. A text. A compliment. Being needed. You pour them in and the water runs out of a hole in the bottom.

The book tells of a man who finally made the money he chased for ten years. He felt "like a fraud." The money went straight through the hole.

## Where the hole comes from

At some point, the book says, you learned "I am not enough on my own." The moment you believe that, the bucket cannot hold anything.

- **Confidence on conditions:** confident if the date goes well, if the outfit gets compliments.
- **A black hole of need:** more texts, more proof, until the other person runs dry.
- **Motion to hide the leak:** if you cannot sit in silence, the book calls that a flow problem.

## Stop pouring

Relationships, the book says, "do not exist to fill your bucket. Relationships exist to share the overflow." So the answer is not a faster supply. It is to stop pouring, let the bucket go empty, and sit with the quiet. The book admits this is the hardest part.

> **Pro tip from the app:** notice one thing you reach for to feel okay, like checking for a reply. Wait ten minutes before you reach. Watch what comes up.

> **Result:** you can feel "just okay" without a fresh cup from outside, and that is the start of a bucket that holds.

## The bottom line

More water does not fix a hole. The book asks you to stop pouring and seal it from the inside.', 6, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l07', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The closed bucket', 'lesson', NULL, 'Some people never cry at work. They never send a late-night text. They cut off anyone who brings drama. They look perfect. The book says they may be the most blocked of all.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, The Bucket Model: The Closed Bucket.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How a closed bucket looks from outside and feels from inside.
- Why the lid cannot keep out pain without keeping out joy.
- How to open it without a crowbar.

## Calm, and hollow

The lid keeps the bad things out. But the book says the lid "cannot differentiate." It blocks the sadness and the joy. You go on a date with a wonderful person and feel nothing. A "quiet, hollow dial tone."

The book tells of Julian, who said he protected his peace at all costs. The book''s reply: "You are not protecting your peace. You are enforcing a quarantine."

- **The Teflon bucket:** love bounces off, and the person who loves you feels shut out.
- **Chasing extremes:** only something loud gets through the lid.
- **Knowing it is not removing it:** you can explain your walls well and still keep them.

## Do not grab a crowbar

High achievers want to force it open. The book says no. Drill "a microscopic hole." Let the system breathe.

And when it opens, it may sting, like blood coming back to a numb foot. Let it tingle. Let the tears come. Do not put the lid back on.

> **Pro tip from the app:** the book gives a check. If your first thought is "Well, I''m just highly logical, there''s nothing wrong with that," pause there. That pride may be the lid.

> **Careful:** a note from the app. Opening up old feeling can be a lot. Go slowly, and lean on a professional or someone you trust.

> **Result:** you can tell calm from closed, and you know the first step is small.

## The bottom line

A lid that keeps out pain keeps out joy too. Open it a little, and let it thaw.', 7, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l08', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Emotion is energy in motion', 'lesson', NULL, 'You get a text: "We need to talk." Before you even think, your chest tightens. The book asks what that feeling actually is.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Energy in Storage: Emotion as Motion.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The book''s plain definition of an emotion.
- How a feeling becomes stored.
- Why the book says there are no good or bad emotions.

## A wire, not a battery

The book compares you to a wire. Electricity moves through a wire. The wire does not hold it. Your body is meant to be a conduit for feeling.

Emotion, the book says, is "energy in motion." It rises, peaks and fades, if you let it.

## How storage happens

At work you feel anger, but you want to be polite. So you swallow it. The anger does not leave. It goes into storage.

- **Stored anger:** the book says it becomes resentment.
- **Stored fear:** it becomes anxiety.
- **Replaying the memory:** you re-trigger the feeling and trap it again, on a loop.

## Stop judging the weather

We call joy good and sadness bad. The book asks you to drop that. All of it is just water. It only becomes toxic when it stops moving. Moving water stays clean. Still water breeds mosquitoes.

So when a feeling comes, you let it be there. You breathe. You let the current pass through the wire. It peaks. It fades. You stay clear.

> **Pro tip from the app:** next time a strong feeling hits, try to name only where it sits in your body, not why it came. Chest, throat or stomach.

> **Result:** you begin to treat feelings as weather that passes, not a verdict on who you are.

## The bottom line

Feelings are meant to move through you. Trouble starts when they get stored.', 8, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l09', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Healing as flushing', 'lesson', NULL, 'You think of healing as a big project. A retreat. A breakthrough. The book says it is closer to brushing your teeth.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Energy in Storage: Healing as Flushing.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why the word "healing" can be a trap.
- What flushing looks like in the body.
- Why it is daily upkeep, not a one-time event.

## Not sick, just full

The word healing, the book says, makes you feel sick and damaged. But you are not an empty house that needs more furniture. You are a full house that needs clearing.

## The mind cannot flush the body

You do not psychoanalyze a blocked pipe. The book tells of Marcus, who managed his anxiety with books, apps and retreats for a decade. When he stopped managing it and let the knot burn, he cried for twenty minutes in a parking lot. Then he felt clear.

A flush, the book says, can look like:

- **Tears.**
- **A heavy sigh you cannot hold back.**
- **A short wave of deep tiredness,** like after a problem is suddenly solved.

## Every night, a little

Healing as flushing is not a one-time event. The book describes a nightly sweep: locate the small annoyance at a coworker, feel it, let it out, drop it. The insecurity from scrolling. Feel it. Drop it.

"Every single night," like brushing your teeth.

> **Pro tip from the app:** keep it small. One minute before sleep to find one leftover feeling and let it go is better than one huge cry a year.

> **Careful:** a note from the app. A big release can feel strong. If it ever feels like too much, stop and reach out to a professional or someone you trust.

> **Result:** you have a daily habit of emptying, so the backlog stops growing.

## The bottom line

Healing is not adding something new. It is letting out what was stored, a little every day.', 9, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l10', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'States attract states', 'lesson', NULL, 'You write a list of what you want in a partner. Then you keep falling for the opposite. The book says your list does not choose. Your bucket does.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Energy in Storage: States Attract States.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why the people you attract match what you carry.
- How each bucket shows up at work and in love.
- Why a new city will not fix an old pattern.

## The tuning fork

The book compares stored feeling to a tuning fork. You walk into a room and your fork finds the one that hums at the same pitch. You feel a pull and call it chemistry.

The book tells of Sarah. A kind, steady man asked her out, and her nervous system panicked. There was no chaos to manage. The faces of her past partners changed. The pattern did not.

## What each bucket attracts

- **A full bucket:** people who bond over shared stress, and people who feed on it.
- **A leaky bucket:** bosses who underpay you and friends who call only for favours.
- **A hidden weight at work:** the book says an interviewer can feel a gap between confident words and heavy energy.

## Change the object, not the mirror

"You cannot hold what you are not calibrated for," the book says. Want better people? Clear the need that keeps you tied to the old ones. If you move to a new city with a full bucket, you rebuild the same life on new streets.

> **Pro tip from the app:** list the last three people who drained you. Write what they had in common. That pattern is a clue to what you are carrying.

> **Result:** you stop blaming luck for a pattern and start working on what you can change.

## The bottom line

Your state chooses before your list does. Empty the bucket and the people around you change.', 10, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l11', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Relationships are plumbing', 'lesson', NULL, 'You probably ask one person to be everything: partner, therapist, best friend, guide. The book says that is a design fault.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, The 7 Relationships Pyramid: The Relationship Architecture.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why the book calls relationships "a plumbing system."
- The game the book uses to explain roles.
- What happens when one pipe carries everything.

## Lanes and roles

The book uses the game Dota. Five players. Each has a role. A Carry deals damage late in the game. A Support keeps the Carry alive. If the Support tries to play Carry, the team loses.

Your relationships work the same way, the book says. Each one is built to process one kind of load. That is why it calls them "a plumbing system."

## One pipe, all the weight

We demand that one or two people process our whole backlog. We want a partner to be therapist, best friend, money adviser and spiritual guide. At first it feels like deep connection. Then the pipe bursts.

- **"Finite beings cannot absorb infinite emotional weight."**
- **No human can handle your full emotional load.**
- **Each relationship has a job,** and it does that job best.

## In the app

The book''s seven relationships are the app''s seven pillars: servitude, friends, family, love, yourself, teachers and faith. Each pillar is its own pyramid in the app. The book draws all seven as one pyramid. The next lessons follow the book''s picture.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write down who you take your heaviest worries to. If one name carries almost everything, that pipe is overloaded.

> **Result:** you can see where you are asking one person to do the work of seven.

## The bottom line

Every relationship is a pipe with one job. Spread the load, and each pipe flows.', 11, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l12', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The seven levels and their Flows', 'lesson', NULL, 'The book builds one pyramid out of seven relationships. Each has a level and a kind of flow it handles.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, The 7 Relationships Pyramid, Levels 1 to 7.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The seven levels, in the book''s order.
- The Flow each level carries.
- The one-word job the book gives each.

## The seven, from the base up

In the book, Level 1 is the foundation at the bottom and Level 7 is the top.

1. **Divine, EnergyFlow.** The Source, the Sink and the Regulator. The book''s word: Surrender.
2. **Guru, MindFlow.** A teacher, mentor, coach or guide who untangles the knot. Clarity.
3. **Self, SkillFlow.** Your own daily irritations, handled by you. Autonomy.
4. **Lover, HeartFlow.** The delicate pipe of being fully seen. Intimacy.
5. **Family, HealthFlow.** Your roots and your body''s sense of safety. Roots.
6. **Friends, FunFlow.** Light, playful circulation. Play.
7. **Servant Leader, TimeFlow, MoneyFlow and WorkFlow.** Your output to the world. Work and money.

## How to read it

The book is clear that a Guru does not need robes. It is anyone or any system that helps you think straight. And family is "mud." Mud grows things, the book says, "but you don''t drink it." Let family be family.

At the top, Servant Leader means you "serve the market" and "lead your projects" without asking the market to prove your worth.

## A note on order

The app lists the pillars with Servitude first. The book''s Level 1 is the Divine. Same seven, a different order.

> **Pro tip from the app:** next to each level, write one person or practice that fills it in your life now. Leave a blank where you have none. The blanks matter.

> **Result:** you have your own map of the seven, with the empty levels in plain sight.

## The bottom line

Seven levels, seven jobs. Your life flows when each one does its own.', 12, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l13', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Bottom-Up Rule', 'lesson', NULL, 'What do you build your life on? A job? A partner? The book says most of us build the pyramid upside down.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, The Bottom-Up Rule and The Inverted Pyramid.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Bottom-Up Rule.
- What the inverted pyramid looks like.
- The Romance Trap and the domino effect of neglect.

## Heavy at the base

The book''s rule: the foundation, Levels 1 to 3 (Divine, Guru, Self), must be the strongest pipes. They take the heavy loads. The top, Levels 5 to 7 (Family, Friends, Servant Leader), take light loads.

## The inverted pyramid

Society, the book says, tells you to start at the top. Work hard. Make money. Find your identity in your job. So you plant the tip of the pyramid in the ground and try to balance your life on it.

But your career "is just a pipe. An output pipe." It was never built to give your life meaning.

- **The Romance Trap:** you build on Level 4 and make a partner your foundation. When they buckle, you do not just lose a person. You lose your base.
- **Anatomy of a collapse:** the book tells of Marcus, who hated being alone and took no advice. When the weight of an unprocessed life landed on the top pipes, they broke.
- **The domino effect:** a broken Level 1 spreads up. You distrust mentors, you crowdsource every choice from friends.

## Society rewards the tip

There is no award for a quiet, steady sense of self, the book notes. Society only rewards the top: the title, the bank balance.

> **Pro tip from the app:** ask yourself what you lean on most when life gets hard. If it is your job or one person, that is your tip in the ground.

> **Result:** you can see whether your own pyramid stands on its base or its point.

## The bottom line

Put the heavy weight at the base and keep the top light. Then the pyramid stands.', 13, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l14', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The seven processing centres', 'lesson', NULL, 'Each level in the book''s pyramid takes in one kind of load and trains one strength. Learn the pairs and you know where each weight goes.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Processing Centers.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The load and the strength for each of the seven.
- How the Self handles everyday friction.
- Why "shallow" can be a good thing.

## Load and strength

- **Divine, the Infinite Center:** load, existential weight. Strength, Surrender.
- **Guru, the Clarity Center:** load, mental friction. Strength, Discernment. A good Guru holds up a mirror.
- **Self, the Baseline Center:** load, daily friction. Strength, Autonomy. A rude email, burnt dinner, a car cutting you off.
- **Lover, the Intimacy Center:** load, vulnerability. Strength, Resonance. Two tuning forks at one pitch.
- **Family, the Grounding Center:** load, belonging. Strength, Acceptance.
- **Friends, the Agility Center:** load, stuck energy. Strength, Lightness. Play.
- **Servant Leader, the Output Center:** load, creative tension. Strength, Impact, through doing the work.

## Two lines worth keeping

On family, the book is simple: "You go to dinner. You eat the food. You talk about the weather." And you let that be enough.

On friends: "Superficiality is not a sin." When your base is deep, light fun is a needed joy.

## A story of the wrong pipe

The book tells of Elena, who brought her whole backlog home to James every night. James was kind. But a Lover cannot do the work of a Self. Over time, it broke them both.

> **Pro tip from the app:** when something weighs on you today, name its load. Daily friction? Take it to yourself. Existential fear? Take it to the base.

> **Result:** you can sort a weight before you hand it to someone, so the right centre handles it.

## The bottom line

Each centre has a load and a strength. Put the right weight in the right place.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l15', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Trace the pipe down', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your work is causing panic. So you read another book on business. The book says you are looking in the wrong place.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, The Upward Flow and The Diagnostic.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What happens when you build from the base up.
- The book''s diagnostic: trace the pipe down.
- Why the pain is rarely where it shows.

## The upward flow

When you follow the Bottom-Up Rule, the book says, things change on the way up. A strong line to the Source. A clear map from a Guru. A steady Self. Then love becomes playful, not life-or-death. Family becomes a place you can visit. Work becomes clean output.

## The diagnostic

When a top pipe hurts, the book says, "trace it down."

1. Is your career causing burnout? Do not start with strategy.
2. Are your friends draining you?
3. Is your home life chaotic?
4. Are you hollow inside?
5. Keep going down until you find the dry pipe.

"It is not luck. It is not karma. It is not your personality. It is plumbing."

## Put the pyramid down

The book ends the chapter with a picture. Stop trying to hold up the sky. Put the pyramid down and let it rest on its base.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick the one area of life that hurts most right now. Write the level below it, then the one below that. Ask what is missing at each step.

> **Result:** you have a habit of looking one level down before you try to fix the place that hurts.

## The bottom line

Pain shows up at the top. Its cause usually sits lower. Trace the pipe down.', 15, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l16', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Sink, Source and Regulator', 'lesson', NULL, 'Some weights are too big for any one person. The fear of death. The grief of a deep loss. The book says you need something bigger to hold them.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, The Spiritual Infrastructure: God as Infrastructure.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three jobs the book gives the Divine.
- The false gods of the modern world.
- The math of surrender.

## Three jobs

The book treats the Divine not as a belief but as infrastructure, like a power grid. It has three jobs.

- **Sink:** the ocean that takes the weight you cannot hold. Rage, shame, grief.
- **Source:** clean energy that comes in from outside you, when your own is low.
- **Regulator:** it absorbs the shock, so the lights stay on when life jolts.

## False gods

When the weight gets too heavy, the book says, we look for a bucket. A partner. Work. Our own body. We make them our God. But they are finite. The pressure breaks them.

## The math of surrender

To plug in, you admit one truth: "You are finite." You are small. Your capacity is limited. The book says this is not an insult. It is a relief.

The book points to the 12 Steps. They called it a Higher Power, didn''t define it, and asked people to use it.

## A daily utility

"Your Spiritual Infrastructure is not a one-time fix. It is a daily utility."

> **Pro tip from the app:** whatever word you use, faith, God, the universe, the book''s point is the same. Name one practice that lets you hand over what is too big.

> **Result:** you know where the heaviest weight goes, so it stops landing on the people you love.

## The bottom line

Some weight is too big for any person. Hand it to something bigger, every day.', 16, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l17', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Guru: choose one map', 'lesson', NULL, 'You watch a hundred videos on psychology and success. You know a lot. You still feel lost. The book says you have too many maps.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, MindFlow and the Guru.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the book means by a Guru.
- Information versus wisdom.
- The three marks of a good Guru system.

## A Guru is a map

Forget the man in robes on a mountain, the book says. A Guru is "a mapmaker." It can be a teacher, a coach, or a whole system of thought. Stoicism. Taoism. The Bhagavad Gita. Buddhism. The 12 Steps. Even this book.

Raw energy without a channel is like raw electricity dumped into a house. It burns. A Guru lays the tracks so your mind''s energy goes somewhere useful.

## Information is not wisdom

"Wisdom is choosing one solid, proven map, and throwing the rest away." A real Guru system gives you fewer things to think about, not more.

The book tells of David, a software engineer who read every book on failure after losing his company and marriage. Knowing more did not move the energy.

## Three marks of a good system

- **It empties the bucket.**
- **It reduces the ego.**
- **It survives pressure.**

The book also says the hard part is submission: follow the map until it is automatic. "The Guru is the architect. The Divine is the funder. The mind just lays the bricks."

> **Pro tip from the app:** list the sources you take advice from. Keep the one that passes all three marks. Pause the rest for a month.

> **Result:** you have one map to follow, and far less noise in your head.

## The bottom line

More information is not more clarity. Choose one good map and follow it.', 17, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l18', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The four-step flush', 'lesson', NULL, 'This is the tool the book asks you to memorise. "You will use it for the rest of your life."

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, The Art of Surrender: The Mechanics of the Flush.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The four steps of the flush.
- Conditional versus absolute surrender.
- What to do with the empty feeling after.

## Hold a glass of water

Hold a glass of water for a minute. Easy. For an hour, your arm aches. For a day, the book says, it is paralysed. The weight did not change. The holding did.

## The four steps

1. **Stop Moving.** Your urge is to run, argue or analyse. Sit down. Breathe.
2. **Locate the Mass.** Do not ask why. Find it in the body. A tight throat. A burning chest. Give it a shape and a weight.
3. **Acknowledge Capacity.** Say: "This is an infinite weight. I am a finite being. My bucket is not big enough to hold this."
4. **The Hand-Off.** Breathe out and slide it down the pipe. "I hand this over to the infrastructure. I release this to the Sink. Take it."

## No conditions

"I will let go of this anger, as soon as they apologize" is a conditional surrender. The book says it keeps the weight. The book tells of Marcus, who let go of a decade of anger "just as an experiment," and slept through the night for the first time in years.

Surrender is not saying what happened was okay. The lesson is kept. The weight is flushed.

## The void

After a big flush, you may feel empty and strange. The book says do not fill it. Do not pick a fight or start scrolling. Sit in the void. Clean water takes a moment to arrive.

> **Pro tip from the app:** practise on something small first, like a sharp comment from today. Learn the four steps on a light weight.

> **Careful:** a note from the app. If a weight is about harm, danger or deep grief, use the flush beside real help, never instead of it.

> **Result:** you have a four-step way to put down what you cannot carry.

## The bottom line

Stop. Locate. Acknowledge. Hand it off. Then sit in the quiet that follows.', 18, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l19', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The daily flush', 'lesson', NULL, 'A big flush can change a year. The book says it still is not enough. Life pours water in every day.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Strategic Vulnerability and The Daily Flush.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why surrender is daily hygiene.
- The oak and the grass.
- What an empty bucket does for the people around you.

## Not an event

"Surrender is not an event... It is a daily hygiene practice." Every email, every conversation, every piece of news adds water. Some is clean. Much is not. So you use the Sink every day.

## The oak and the grass

The book''s picture of strategic vulnerability: a storm hits. The stiff oak resists and snaps. The grass bends, lets the wind pass, and stands back up. Being willing to be empty is what makes you hard to break.

## What changes

The book says an empty bucket shows.

- **You are patient,** because there is room for what others bring.
- **You are easy to be around,** because states attract states.
- **You can act,** because the clean energy has somewhere to go.

That last point leads into the next chapter. Clean energy that just sits, the book warns, goes stale. It needs an outlet in skill, love and work.

> **Pro tip from the app:** tie a one-minute flush to something you already do daily, like the first sip of tea. Small and daily beats big and rare.

> **Result:** you keep the bucket near empty instead of waiting for it to overflow.

## The bottom line

Flush a little every day. Bend like the grass, and stand back up.', 19, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l20', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'SkillFlow: the Self', 'lesson', NULL, '"Find yourself." "Love yourself." You have heard it all. The book says the Self is not something to find. It is something you do.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Self-Relationship and SkillFlow.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What SkillFlow is.
- What a blocked SkillFlow looks like.
- How the book says you reclaim your core.

## Overloaded, not empty

Think of the times you said "I don''t know what I want." The book asks: were you empty, or overloaded? Every time you lose your sense of self, it says, you are carrying too much.

## SkillFlow

SkillFlow is how you learn, build, create and master a craft. When it is blocked, it "looks like procrastination… perfectionism… imposter syndrome."

Why do you avoid the work? The work stirs a feeling. Fear of failure, of judgment. That feeling hits a full bucket. So you stop.

## The outsourced identity

When the Self is clogged, the book says, you outsource it. You look to a partner to fill the void. It turns into "a hostage situation." A Lover cannot fix a missing Self.

## Ride the bicycle

The book''s picture is learning to ride a bike. You grip the bars and wobble. Then you relax and it flows. Do the same with feelings. Do not fight the fear. Let it pass, and keep pedalling.

- **Mastery is not ego:** a master at work is not thinking how great they are.
- **A mistake is data:** it does not shatter you.
- **SkillFlow demands output:** you grow by making, not by planning.

> **Pro tip from the app:** when you avoid a task, write the feeling it stirs in one word. Then do five minutes of the task with the feeling still there.

> **Result:** you can work through fear instead of waiting for it to leave first.

## The bottom line

A strong Self is a clear one. Let the fear move, and keep making.', 20, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l21', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'HeartFlow: two rivers', 'lesson', NULL, '"You complete me." It sounds like devotion. The book calls it a structural disaster.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, HeartFlow and the Lover.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why "you complete me" overloads love.
- The book''s picture of two rivers side by side.
- What vulnerability really is.

## Love versus utility

When you bring your whole backlog to a partner and say "Here. You hold this," the book says you are not seeing them. You are using them. It feels like falling in love, because the weight is gone for a while.

Then the honeymoon ends. The backlog you never flushed fills the bucket again. Their quirks stop being cute. Jealousy, the book says, "is a supply chain issue." It is fear that your main valve might leave.

## Two rivers

HeartFlow is two rivers that run side by side. Each has its own source. They share the view. Neither drains into the other.

The book tells of David and Sarah, five years married and close to the end. David learned to handle his own flow. By week three, Sarah''s system began to drain too, and the warmth came back.

## Vulnerability, redefined

Vulnerability is not dumping chaos onto your partner. It is letting yourself be seen while you carry your own weight. "A companion in the flow. Not a plumber for the system."

> **Pro tip from the app:** before you bring a heavy day to your partner, ask: am I sharing this, or handing it over to be fixed?

> **Result:** you can love from a lighter bucket, so small things stay small.

## The bottom line

Love works best between two whole people. Run beside each other. Do not drain into each other.', 21, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l22', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'When intimacy collapses', 'lesson', NULL, 'Relationships rarely end in one blow-up. The book says they end with a slow, silent build-up of pressure.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, The Collapse of Intimacy.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The weight distribution problem.
- What resentment is, in the book''s view.
- Why a weekend away cannot fix an overloaded core.

## Too much in one glass

You ask a partner to give you purpose and manage your daily anxiety. You drop the heavy gears of work and self into the Lover pipe. For a while it bends. Then it breaks.

## The myth of bad communication

"Communication is just the transfer of water from one bucket to another." If both buckets are full, talking more just moves dirty water.

## Resentment

The book calls resentment "the anger of a trapped system realizing its escape route is blocked." You gave them your weight. They dropped it. Now you carry it again.

- **The roommate phase:** touch feels like a threat, not love.
- **The weekend getaway:** it cannot fix an overloaded core.
- **No villains:** in Elena and James''s story, the book says, "there was only a misunderstanding of physics."

## Sit in the ruins

After a break-up, the book says, do not run to a dating app, a bottle or endless work. You are meeting your own backlog. Sit with it. Learn to process your own reality.

> **Pro tip from the app:** if you are in a hard patch, drop the question "whose fault is it?" Ask "what weight is in the wrong pipe?"

> **Careful:** a note from the app. If a relationship involves fear for your safety, this lesson is not the answer. Reach out for real help now.

> **Result:** you can look at a hard relationship without blame, and see what each person is carrying.

## The bottom line

Love breaks under weight it was never built to carry. Take back your own weight first.', 22, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l23', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Family as roots', 'lesson', NULL, 'You walk through the door at home and your body braces. Nobody has said anything yet. The book says family lives in the body.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, HealthFlow and Family.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why the book ties family to health.
- What "accept their incapacity" means.
- The family flush.

## Roots and the body

The book places HealthFlow, NutriFlow and BodyFlow under Family. Your family built your nervous system. If energy from home does not move out, the book says, "it moves down." Into the gut, the joints, the shoulders.

## Accept their incapacity

Cutting family off does not empty the bucket, the book says. The weight just moves to another pipe.

So the book asks you to accept their incapacity. "When you expect a drowning person to hand you a towel, you are going to be disappointed." It is not that they don''t want to. They can''t.

## Food and posture

- **NutriFlow:** reaching for cookies after a hard call with a parent. The book says you are hungry for steadiness, not sugar.
- **BodyFlow:** rounded shoulders, a clenched jaw. You cannot pull your shoulders back for long while a weight pulls them down.

## The family flush

When a comment at home lands, do not fight back. Feel the tightness. Say to yourself: "This is my backlog. This is the weight I have been carrying. I don''t need to carry it anymore." The book calls family "the gym" for your calm.

> **Pro tip from the app:** at the next family meal, aim for the book''s simple goal. Eat the food. Talk about the weather. Let that be enough.

> **Careful:** a note from the app. If home is not safe, accepting is not the task. Protect yourself first and reach out for help.

> **Result:** you can be with family without taking on their weight.

## The bottom line

Family is your root, not your fix. Accept them, and keep your own bucket clear.', 23, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l24', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'FunFlow: friends are for play', 'lesson', NULL, 'Every Thursday you meet a friend for dinner. For two hours, you both unload. Over time you start to dread Thursdays. The book explains why.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, FunFlow and Friendship.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The venting trap.
- What friends are actually for, in the book''s view.
- The friendship flush.

## The venting trap

Venting feels healthy. The book says it is "stirring" dirty water with a stick. It gets cloudy. It does not leave. The Thursday dinner becomes "a dumping ground," and two full buckets splash each other.

## Friends are for FunFlow

"Fun is not a luxury." Friends, the book says, are the pressure valves of the system. They exist for play. A real laugh makes the guard step away from the door.

And the "shallow" friend who jokes about the waiter? For one second, the loop of suffering breaks. That is the job.

## Move the body

Venting happens when two people sit still and stare at each other. The book''s fix: move. "Let''s go play pool." "Let''s go for a hike." "Let''s go to an arcade."

## The friendship flush

When a friendship feels heavy, the book says, inject pure play. Send a ridiculous meme. Share a dumb memory from ten years ago. Ask a pointless question: "If you could only eat one food for the rest of your life, what is it?"

> **Pro tip from the app:** next time you meet a friend, plan one thing to do, not just a table to sit at.

> **Result:** your friendships feel lighter, and you leave them with more energy than you came with.

## The bottom line

Friends are for play, not for processing your whole backlog. Move, laugh, and let the valve open.', 24, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l25', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'MoneyFlow: consumer or channel', 'lesson', NULL, 'You are exhausted. You wake up dreading Monday. You think you need a holiday. The book says you do not.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, MoneyFlow and Leadership.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why work cannot be your mirror.
- The Consumer versus the Channel.
- The work and money flush.

## Work is an output pipe

Your job, the book says, is "a mechanical structure for delivering value." It is not a mirror for your soul. Ask it for identity and losing it feels like dying.

- **The proxy boss:** your mind may treat a boss like a parent, and replay old fear.
- **Burnout:** the book says most of your energy goes to managing fear and resentment, not work. "You do not need a vacation."

## Consumer or Channel

A Consumer extracts. It must win, hoard and push. A Channel lets value pass through. Money, the book says, is "Currency. Current. Flow." Receive it with thanks. Release it with intention, not panic.

## Service is the hack

When you feel low, your focus turns inward. The book''s answer: "Find one person. And solve a problem for them."

## The work and money flush

1. Look at your bank balance.
2. Notice the feeling in your body. Do not try to change the number.
3. Say: "This is just a measurement. It is not my worth. It is not my safety."
4. Walk into work as a channel, not a consumer.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in the app, this is your Servitude pillar. Today, add one task that solves a real problem for one person.

> **Result:** you can face money and work without fear deciding for you.

## The bottom line

Be a channel. Let value pass through you, and let money be a measure, not your worth.', 25, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l26', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Daily Emptying Ritual', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most morning routines are about getting more: more focus, more energy. The book''s daily ritual is about letting go.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Living in Perpetual Flow: The Daily Emptying Ritual.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three phases of the ritual.
- How long each one takes.
- The words the book gives you for the end of the day.

## Three phases

"The Morning Flush. The Midday Filter. The Evening Drain."

## The Morning Flush, about three minutes

- **Do not reach for the phone.** Lie still.
- **Find the weight.** Chest, stomach, jaw.
- **Drop the story.** The moment you say "I am anxious because I have that big meeting today," you trap it.
- **Breathe into it.** Easy breaths, like water over a dirty plate.

## The Midday Filter, about ninety seconds

A rude email arrives. Heat rises in your neck. Step away from the screen. Notice the body. Let the shoulders drop. Do not fix the boss in your head. The book says a chemical surge passes in about ninety seconds if you stop pressing the button.

## The Evening Drain

No screens. No music. Ask: "What am I still carrying from today?" Then hand each weight to its level.

- **A fear about the future:** to the Divine.
- **Confusion about your path:** to the Guru.
- **Frustration with yourself:** to the Self.

Then say: "I am done with this day. I am returning this energy. I choose to sleep empty."

> **Pro tip from the app:** in the app, add the three phases as one repeating task on Daily, with a time for the evening one.

> **Result:** you go to bed with an empty bucket, and wake up without yesterday''s weight.

## The bottom line

Morning flush. Midday filter. Evening drain. Every day.', 26, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l27', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Monitoring the system', 'lesson', NULL, 'A man fixes a crack in his ceiling. It comes back bigger. He hires a better plasterer. It comes back again. The book says he never looked at the ground.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Monitoring the System.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The house with the cracked ceiling.
- The book''s three-step diagnostic.
- Why the root is almost always in the bottom three.

## The crack is not the problem

The ground under the house was shifting. The ceiling was just "the weakest point," where the deep tension showed up. Life is the same. When a relationship or job breaks, it exposes the system, not the person.

## Lane by lane

The book walks through each lane. A partner you suddenly need to text back every hour may be holding fear that belongs to the Divine. A job you hate may be carrying a need for meaning that belongs to the Guru. Friends full of drama may be catching jealousy you cannot face yourself.

## The three-step diagnostic

1. **Locate the Break.** Name which lane hurts.
2. **Remove the Blame.** Stop asking "Who did this to me?" Ask "Where is the pressure coming from?"
3. **Look One Level Down.** If the Self is failing, look at the Divine. "Keep walking down the stairs until you find the dry pipe."

The book says you will almost always find the root in the bottom three lanes.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in the app, a weekly review is a good time to run the three steps on whatever felt heaviest that week.

> **Result:** you stop being afraid of things breaking, because you know how to trace the leak.

## The bottom line

Find the break, drop the blame, and look one level down.', 27, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-l28', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'The Abundance Default', 'lesson', NULL, 'We think abundance means having more. The book says that is just hoarding.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Abundance Default.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why accumulation is not abundance.
- The dam and the river.
- The question the book ends on.

## Holding nothing

"Accumulation is not abundance. Accumulation is just hoarding." The Abundance Default, the book says, "is not a state of having everything. It is a state of holding nothing."

## David''s dam

The book tells of David, who built his wealth like a dam. Huge, full and tense, because if it broke it would destroy everything. He was not wealthy. He was a prisoner. When he let the water move, the tightness in his chest left and he slept again. "He was no longer a dam. He was a river."

## How it changes a day

- **Love:** you stop using your partner as a water supply.
- **Money:** "Currency. Current. Flow." A lost client brings a short sting, then it passes.
- **Friends:** you feel the sting of not being invited, and you do not store it.
- **Feelings:** the book chooses stability over happiness. You cannot freeze a spike of joy. With an empty bucket, there is room for sadness too, and it passes.

## The last question

"What would you do today, if you had nothing left to prove?"

The quiet may feel boring at first, the book admits. Accept it. "Keep the bottom lanes clear. Keep the valve open."

> **Pro tip from the app:** answer the book''s question in one line, and add one task from your answer to Daily.

> **Result:** you finish the course with the whole method in one habit: let it in, feel it, let it go.

## The bottom line

Abundance is flow, not storage. Feel every drop, then step out of the way.', 28, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO course_assignments (id, program_id, title, instructions, rubric, due_days, sequence) VALUES ('prog-book-relationship-method-a01', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Seven days of the Daily Emptying Ritual', 'For seven days, keep the book''s Daily Emptying Ritual: the Morning Flush (about three minutes, before your phone), the Midday Filter (about ninety seconds, once something lands), and the Evening Drain (no screens, "What am I still carrying from today?").

Each evening, write two lines: what you found, and which level you handed it to (Divine, Guru or Self).

On day seven, write a short note: which phase was hardest to keep, and one thing that felt lighter by the end. Never include anything private you do not want a mentor to read.', 'All seven days are logged with the two lines. Each weight is handed to a level that fits the book''s sorting (fear about the future to the Divine, confusion to the Guru, frustration with yourself to the Self). The day-seven note names the hardest phase honestly and one concrete change.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO cohorts (id, program_id, name, start_at, end_at, capacity, status, manager_user_id, created_at) VALUES ('coh-book-relationship-method-open', 'prog-book-relationship-method', 'Self-paced, open all year', '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z', '2027-12-31T00:00:00.000Z', 1000, 'running', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');

-- Dictionary of Mistakes for Creators
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO programs (id, name, description, price_points, price_cents, currency, delivery_mode, published, outcomes, prerequisites, created_by, created_at) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators', 'Dictionary of Mistakes for Creators', 'A course from Shubham Aggarwal''s book Dictionary of Mistakes for Creators. Thirty-two lessons through the eight chapters: the invisible mistake loop, identity, how you speak, consistency, self-doubt, other creators, positioning and money. Each chapter goes awareness, diagnosis, correction, like the book. Every lesson says which chapter it comes from; the pro tips are the app''s own.', 0, 0, 'INR', 'online', 1, 'You can find which loop is holding you back, run the book''s filters before you publish, keep a Resonance Log and an Evidence Vault, build a small pod of peers, write a thesis you can defend, and map a simple offer ladder.', 'None. Useful if you post anything, anywhere, or want to.', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l01', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Doing everything right, still invisible', 'lesson', NULL, 'You post on time. You use the hooks everyone uses. You study the viral posts. And nothing happens.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, The Invisible Mistake Loop: The Myth of Doing Everything Right.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why creators fail quietly, not loudly.
- What the book means by a loop of micro-errors.
- Why best practices can make you blend in.

## Creators fade

"Creators don''t fail loudly. They fail quietly." They post for a week, vanish for three, then come back promising to be consistent. The book says that is not a lack of discipline. "That is a broken loop."

## Micro-errors

The mistakes are small. In identity. In how you speak. In where you stand. None of them sinks your account overnight. They compound quietly until growth stops and energy drains.

- **Running on a treadmill:** you sweat, so you think you are moving.
- **A victim of best practices:** the same rulebook as the rest of the herd gets the same result.
- **A controller that isn''t plugged in:** the book''s picture of pushing buttons that do nothing.

## Clarity, not cameras

You think you need better lighting or a studio. The book says it is not your camera. It is your clarity. People watch low-quality videos all the time when the message is clear.

## Open the hood

The silence is data. It says your current inputs are not working for you. The book asks you to stop forcing a result and audit the habits underneath.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write down three "rules" you follow because everyone says so. Next to each, write whether it has ever worked for you.

> **Result:** you stop reading silence as proof you are bad, and start reading it as a signal to change the inputs.

## The bottom line

Effort is not the problem. The loop is. Find it before you push harder.', 1, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l02', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Six symptoms and the matrix', 'lesson', NULL, 'You cannot fix a glitch you cannot see. The book gives you six symptoms and a four-part matrix to find it.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Diagnosis and The Diagnostic Matrix.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The six symptoms of the loop.
- The four loops in the Diagnostic Matrix.
- One check question for each loop.

## Six symptoms

- **The Engagement Delusion:** reading impressions instead of how deeply people respond.
- **The Chameleon Syndrome:** a new voice every week, so nobody trusts you.
- **The Hedging Habit:** "usually," "maybe," "most of the time." Self-doubt in your sentences.
- **The Metrics Obsession Loop:** judging a seed by checking it every hour.
- **The Isolation Protocol:** seeing other creators only as rivals.
- **The "Start Over" Urge:** a new handle, a new platform, a grand pivot.

## The matrix

The book says your stall hides in one or more of four loops.

1. **Identity.** Does my content sound like me, or like "a marketer wearing my face"?
2. **Communication.** Is my comment section full of one-word replies?
3. **Consistency.** Do I disappear for weeks when I feel uninspired?
4. **Relationships.** Do I view other creators purely as competitors?

The book''s verdicts are sharp. Identity: "performing instead of projecting." Consistency: "confusing emotional discipline with structural leverage."

## Before you pivot

If you want to start over, the book asks: have you truly outgrown the topic, or did it just get hard? Pivoting to escape "resets your compounding interest to zero."

> **Pro tip from the app:** answer the four check questions honestly. Circle the loop with the most yeses. That is where you start.

> **Result:** you know which of the four loops is costing you most.

## The bottom line

Six symptoms, four loops. Name yours before you change anything.', 2, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l03', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The recursive loop', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most creators work in a straight line. Post, hope, post again. The book wants a loop that learns.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Correction: the recursive loop, Steps 1 to 4.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Hard Stop.
- The three-question Output Filter.
- The Calibration Protocol and the Resonance Log.

## Step 1: the Hard Stop

"For the next 72 hours, you are not allowed to publish anything." No threads. No reels. Watch the urge to post, and do not act on it. You cannot read the label from inside the jar.

## Step 2: the filter

Every piece must pass three questions, or you delete it.

1. Does this challenge a default assumption?
2. Could a competitor publish this exact same post?
3. Is this an observation or a declaration?

## Step 3: calibrate

You are a scientist now. The post is the test. Likes and views mean little. The book''s signals:

- **The depth of the reply:** three long comments beat a hundred "great post."
- **The private share:** the bookmark, the forward. The book calls it "the holy grail."
- **The lateral connection:** who your post brings to you.

## Step 4: the Resonance Log

Every week, take your best piece and your worst. For the winner, ask what feeling it hit and what phrase people quoted back. For the loser, ask if it broke the filter or was too broad. Write the answers down.

> **Pro tip from the app:** keep the Resonance Log as a weekly task on Daily, so it happens on the same day each week.

> **Result:** each week starts from what the last one taught you, not from zero.

## The bottom line

Stop. Filter. Calibrate. Log. A loop that learns beats a line that repeats.', 3, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l04', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Engineering friction', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your feedback loop only works if you follow it. The book says willpower will not hold it. Friction will.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Correction: Steps 5 and 6.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three friction tactics.
- How to grow through other people''s comment sections.
- The math of the new loop.

## Friction tactics

- **The Batch and Delay:** "You write on Tuesday. You edit and filter on Wednesday. You publish on Thursday." The writing brain loves its words. The editing brain must be cold. Forty-eight hours lets the attachment fade.
- **The Template Ban:** stop pouring your ideas into borrowed shapes, and build the muscle of saying it your way.
- **The Public Constraint:** do not announce "I am posting every day!" Announce "I am writing a weekly deep-dive on [Specific Premise], published every Sunday." A tight promise filters out the noise.

## Step 6: recalibrate the network

The book treats every comment you leave as a small test. Do not summarise someone''s post. Add a missing piece. A short story. A point they did not make. Their audience sees it, and so do they.

## The math

In the static loop, you post 300 times a year and stay a three out of ten. In the recursive loop, each post starts from the last lesson, so the quality climbs. Then consistency stops being willpower. "It is just the hum of the machine."

> **Pro tip from the app:** put write, edit and publish on three different days in your week. The app''s Plan my week on Daily can help you place them.

> **Result:** your work gets a cold second look before anyone else sees it.

## The bottom line

Build friction into the right places. Write, wait, then publish.', 4, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l05', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The echo chamber', 'lesson', NULL, 'You open the feed for ideas. You find a format that works. So does everyone else. The book calls this the echo chamber.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, Identity and Authenticity Mistakes: The Echo Chamber Trap.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why you cannot out-work a confused identity.
- The four phases of a trend.
- Why burnout comes from working out of line with yourself.

## You cannot out-work it

"You cannot out-work a confused identity." Post twice a day, perfect your thumbnails, and still blend into the background.

## Two layers

The book splits content into two layers. The output is what people see: the words, the format. The input is the hidden layer: your years of experience, your obsessions, your failures. The creators who win have output that matches their input.

## The four phases of a trend

1. **The Originator:** someone with real clarity has a fresh idea. It explodes.
2. **The Early Adopters:** smart creators learn why it worked and make it their own.
3. **The Echo Chamber:** thousands copy the surface without the reason. The feed fills with clones.
4. **The Fatigue:** the audience sees the pattern and scrolls past.

The book says most invisible creators live in phase three.

## Burnout

"Burnout is not caused by working too hard. Burnout is caused by working out of alignment." Wearing a mask takes energy every day.

> **Pro tip from the app:** look at your last ten posts. Mark each one that started from your own life and each one that started from someone else''s format.

> **Result:** you can see how much of your feed is your input, and how much is an echo.

## The bottom line

Stop copying the surface. Your own input is the one thing nobody else has.', 5, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l06', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Eight signs of a fractured persona', 'lesson', NULL, 'You can spot clones on other people''s pages. The book asks you to look for them on your own.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, Diagnosis: Symptoms of a Fractured Persona.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The eight symptoms the book names.
- A quick test you can run on your own feed.
- Why a fractured persona traps you.

## The eight symptoms

- **Translation Exhaustion:** you turn your real thoughts into "creator voice" and it wears you out.
- **Swipe File Dependency:** you cannot start without someone else''s template.
- **Chameleon Feed:** read twenty posts as a stranger. Does it sound like one person or a committee?
- **Hollow Engagement:** "a mile wide and an inch deep." Likes, but no real questions.
- **Post-Publishing Regret:** you would not want a friend or partner to read it aloud.
- **Unscripted Terror:** live or on a podcast, the mask slips.
- **Endless Pivot:** a new trend, a new guru, a new bio.
- **Hostage Metric:** a viral hit you now feel forced to repeat.

## Why it traps you

Expressing your real thoughts should feel like relief, the book says. If publishing feels heavy, you are carrying a mask. And when you grow with a fractured persona, "the audience becomes your warden."

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick the symptom that stung most. Write one sentence about when it shows up for you.

> **Result:** you know which mask you wear, and when you put it on.

## The bottom line

A persona you have to keep up will wear you out. Spot the mask first.', 6, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l07', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Input Fast and your enemy', 'lesson', NULL, 'To find your own voice, the book starts by taking things away.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, Correction: Systems 1 to 3.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The thirty-day Input Fast.
- The Antagonist Protocol.
- The Obsession Intersection.

## System 1: the Input Fast

"For the next thirty days, you are going to starve the echo chamber." No swipe file. No saving viral posts to study. If you need to post, you log in, post and close the app.

The silence in your head will feel scary at first. The book says boredom is what pushes original thought. Read outside your field. Listen to the people around you.

## System 2: the Antagonist Protocol

"I believe in hard work" is not a value. Nobody disagrees with it. The book calls these "bumper stickers."

So start with what you are against. Define the enemy. In copywriting, maybe it is pushy "bro-marketing." In productivity, maybe it is tuning every minute of the day. Once you name what you stand against, you know who you are.

## System 3: the Obsession Intersection

Draw three columns.

1. Your main expertise, the thing people pay you for.
2. A second skill you are unusually good at.
3. "Your weird obsession," the rabbit hole you fall into at 2 AM on a Saturday.

Then use the words of column three to explain the problems of column one. Nobody else has your mix.

> **Pro tip from the app:** fill in the three columns today. Then write one post idea that uses all three.

> **Result:** you have an angle nobody can copy, because nobody else has your columns.

## The bottom line

Starve the echo, name your enemy, and write from the crossing of your obsessions.', 7, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l08', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The Barstool Test and the desert', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your ideas are clear now. Your voice still sounds like a corporate robot. And when you change, the numbers will dip. The book prepares you for both.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, Correction: Systems 4 to 6.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Barstool Test.
- The Engagement Desert and the 90-Day Signal Test.
- The three-question Alignment Filter.

## System 4: the Barstool Test

The book tells of an expert who wrote like a textbook. Then, over a drink, someone said his work was "just common sense." He tore that apart for five minutes, sharp and alive.

So before you publish, read it out loud. Imagine a close friend at a bar. Would you really say these words? "Professionalism is the enemy of resonance."

## System 5: the Engagement Desert

When you drop the templates, your old audience may not engage. Reach drops. The book calls this the Engagement Desert, and says most people turn back here.

The 90-Day Signal Test: for ninety days, look at no vanity numbers. Ask one thing. "Did I tell the truth today?" If yes, "the day is a win."

## System 6: the Alignment Filter

Three questions. Fail one and it goes back to drafts.

1. Does this require translation?
2. Could someone else have written this?
3. Am I protecting my ego?

The book would rather you post two true pieces a week than fourteen watered-down ones.

> **Pro tip from the app:** record yourself explaining your next post out loud, then write from the recording.

> **Result:** your posts sound like you talking, and you have a plan for the dip.

## The bottom line

Write like you talk. Walk through the desert. Filter every piece for truth.', 8, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l09', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Saying too much', 'lesson', NULL, 'You hit record. You start with some background. Then a little context. By the time you reach the point, they are gone.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Communication and Speaking Mistakes: The Curse of Over-Explaining.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why over-explaining feels helpful and is not.
- The red dye in the bathtub.
- What real generosity looks like in content.

## It feels thorough

When you over-explain, you feel helpful. The book says you are not. Your audience has a problem and wants the fix. They do not care about your journey to the fix yet.

## Red dye in a bathtub

Your core idea is a drop of red dye. Your context, your warm-up and your reasons are the bathtub. Drop the dye in the bath and you cannot see it any more.

- **The five-paragraph essay:** it is built for a captive reader. Online, nobody is captive.
- **The nuance illusion:** covering every angle loses the main thread.
- **A wall of text as a shield:** "They just didn''t read the whole thing."

## Generosity is editing

The book says real generosity means doing the hard work of cutting, so the audience does not have to. A comedian gives you just enough setup for the punchline. Not one word more.

And the platforms only measure behaviour: whether people stay. A great idea buried in a warm-up does not get seen.

> **Pro tip from the app:** take your last script. Find the sentence that is the actual point. Count how many words came before it.

> **Result:** you can see how long your audience waits before you give them anything.

## The bottom line

Trust your audience to get it the first time. Say it, then stop.', 9, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l10', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Seven symptoms of muddled messaging', 'lesson', NULL, 'Nobody thinks their own message is muddled. It all makes sense in your head. The book gives you a checklist.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Diagnosis: seven symptoms.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The seven symptoms.
- The test for each one.
- Why a single call to action wins.

## The seven

- **The Self-Centric Hook:** your first line is about you. Count the "I"s in your last ten openers.
- **The Bait and Switch:** a bold hook, then a body that reads like a dictionary. Keep the same energy start to end.
- **The "Frankenstein" Idea:** too many ideas stitched together. Can you sum it up in one sentence?
- **The Language of Insecurity:** search for "I think," "maybe," "just." The book calls this a Weak Word Audit.
- **"Clever Over Clear":** jargon hiding a lack of clarity. Would a tenth grader follow it?
- **The Broken Transition:** you know how two points link, but you never wrote the link.
- **The Desperate Exit:** five calls to action at the end. Pick one.

## Why it matters

Confusion leads to doing nothing. A muddled post may get views and still change nobody''s day.

The book says diagnosis is "a daily practice," not a one-time check.

> **Pro tip from the app:** run just one test today, the Weak Word Audit, on your next draft. Delete every "maybe" you do not need.

> **Result:** you have a seven-point checklist to run on any script before it goes out.

## The bottom line

Clear beats clever. One idea, one energy, one ask.', 10, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l11', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Anchor and the bridge', 'lesson', NULL, 'Trying harder to be concise does not work, the book says. You need rules you follow before you start.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Correction: The Anchor Protocol and The Hook-to-Value Bridge.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to write an anchor sentence.
- The Hook-to-Value Bridge.
- The Kill List of opening phrases.

## The Anchor Protocol

Before you write or record, write one sentence:

"By the end of this [piece of content], the viewer will understand [X] so they can achieve [Y]."

A topic like "productivity" invites rambling. An anchor is a destination. The book''s example: put the phone in another room so you stop hitting snooze. Now the history of sleep has no place in the video, so it goes.

## The Hook-to-Value Bridge

The rule: "The second sentence must validate the first sentence. The first thirty seconds must pay off the title."

No throat-clearing. No "welcome back." The hook makes a promise. The next line pays it.

## The Kill List

If any of these sit in the first fifth of your script, delete them.

- "Before we begin..."
- "A quick background..."
- "I wanted to make this because..."
- "Welcome back..."
- "For context..."

"Make the audience feel like they are jumping onto a moving train."

> **Pro tip from the app:** write the anchor sentence at the top of every draft. Check every paragraph against it before you post.

> **Result:** every piece has a clear destination and starts moving in the first line.

## The bottom line

Anchor first. Pay off the hook at once. Kill the warm-up.', 11, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l12', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Subtract, shape, ask "So what?"', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most creators think editing means polishing. The book says editing is cutting.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Correction: Systems 3 to 6.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The four steps of the Subtraction Algorithm.
- Visual Architecture.
- The "So What?" Filter and the Repetition Engine.

## The Subtraction Algorithm

Write the messy first draft. Then cut.

1. **The Ten Percent Cut.** A 1,000-word script loses 100 words. No choice.
2. **The Preamble Execution.** Delete the three sentences right after your hook. Most of the time it reads better.
3. **The "That" Audit.** Most "that"s slow the reader down.
4. **The Caveat Cleanse.** Search for "I think," "maybe," "usually." Erase them.

## Visual Architecture

People scan before they read. The book''s rule: never write a paragraph longer than three sentences. Use one-line paragraphs for punch. In video, the same idea is pacing: cuts, text on screen, a change of angle.

## The "So What?" Filter

After every claim, ask "So what?" as your most impatient viewer. If you cannot answer with a real benefit, it is noise. Add "which means..." and finish the thought.

## The Repetition Engine

"You do not need 100 different ideas. You need 3 core ideas, explained 100 different ways." What feels like a broken record to you is a clear signal to everyone else.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write your three core ideas at the top of your notes. Tag every new post with the one it serves.

> **Result:** shorter, sharper pieces that all point back to the same few truths.

## The bottom line

Cut ten percent, then ask "So what?" Say your core ideas again and again.', 12, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l13', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Why motivation fails', 'lesson', NULL, 'You read a thread. Everything clicks. You write three posts in one sitting. A week later, nothing. The book calls this the start-stop cycle.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Consistency, Procrastination and Compounding: Why Motivation Is a Failed Strategy.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What procrastination is, in the book''s view.
- Why the start-stop cycle kills compounding.
- Why real consistency is boring.

## Procrastination is avoidance

"Procrastination is not laziness. It is emotional avoidance." When you avoid the camera, you are avoiding a feeling. You call it "gathering inspiration." Deep down, the book says, you know you are hiding.

## The cost of start-stop

Every swing between bursts and silence costs you compounding. And compounding, the book says, "is the only force that actually builds leverage." You leave the field right before the harvest.

## Boring on purpose

Do you wait for motivation to brush your teeth? No. True consistency "looks like a quiet room, a cup of coffee, and a blinking cursor."

- **Visible creators are not more disciplined:** they built a system that does not need a burst.
- **A bad sentence will not kill you:** stopping will.
- **The wall gets built either way:** one brick a day.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick a fixed time for creating, and add it to Daily as a repeating task. Same time, every day it is set.

> **Result:** you stop waiting for the feeling and start showing up on a schedule.

## The bottom line

Motivation comes and goes. A boring system keeps the wall going up.', 13, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l14', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The five friction phases', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your process leaks somewhere between the idea and the post. The book maps the five places it usually leaks.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Diagnosis: the five friction phases and the Micro-Friction Audit.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The five phases where work stalls.
- The Micro-Friction Audit.
- The three seconds that decide everything.

## The five phases

1. **The Idea Trap.** An idea in your head stays perfect. A drafted one can fail. So it never leaves your notes.
2. **The Research Camouflage.** You tell yourself you are just doing a quick search. Forty-five minutes later you are deep in a podcast.
3. **The Blank Page Standoff.** You write a sentence. It sounds bad. The cursor blinks.
4. **The Perfectionism Shield.** Your taste is ahead of your skill. You refuse to post until it feels profound.
5. **The Publishing Paralysis.** You schedule it for next week, then delete it as "outdated."

## The Micro-Friction Audit

These breaks happen in fractions of a second. The book''s author recorded his own screen for a day to catch them. He found he lost hours to small escapes the moment writing got hard.

## Three seconds

The book describes the moment after a weak headline when the cursor froze, and his hand moved to open a new tab. "If you can survive those three seconds without clicking away, you win."

> **Pro tip from the app:** for one work session, keep a sheet beside you. Every time you leave the task, write a tick and what you went to.

> **Result:** you know which of the five phases is your leak, and what you reach for when it hits.

## The bottom line

Find the exact moment you escape. That is where the system needs a guardrail.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l15', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Idea Triage and the Knowledge Diet', 'lesson', NULL, 'Three hundred ideas in your notes do not make you creative. The book says they make you stuck.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Correction: Protocols 1 and 2.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three containers of Idea Triage.
- Just-in-time learning.
- The Placeholder Rule.

## Protocol 1: Idea Triage

Use only three containers.

1. **The Inbox.** Notes app, notebook or voice memo. Ideas live here for 48 hours at most.
2. **The Assembly Line.** It holds three ideas at most. "Not four. Not ten." If an idea moves here, you start writing it now.
3. **The Archive.** Where ideas go when they do not make the cut.

With three active ideas, you cannot hide behind endless choice. "Execute one, or admit you are choosing not to work."

## Protocol 2: the Knowledge Diet

"Research is the most socially acceptable form of procrastination." Most of us learn just in case. The book asks you to learn just in time: only what the piece in front of you needs.

## The Placeholder Rule

When you hit a gap mid-draft, do not open a new tab. Type `[TK]` or "[INSERT STAT HERE]" and keep writing. Only when the whole draft is done do you open the browser, find the one fact, paste it, and close it.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in the app, you can treat the Assembly Line as three tasks on Daily. A fourth idea waits until one is done.

> **Result:** fewer ideas in play, more of them finished.

## The bottom line

Capture freely. Work on three. Learn only what today''s draft needs.', 15, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l16', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Blackout Box, 80%, firewall', 'lesson', NULL, 'Drafting, editing and publishing need three different states of mind. The book gives each one a protocol.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Correction: Protocols 3 to 5.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Blackout Box.
- The Ship at 80% Mandate and its two passes.
- The Emotional Firewall.

## Protocol 3: the Blackout Box

The book''s author once bought a cheap old laptop and pulled out its Wi-Fi card. With no escape, the discomfort peaked, then broke. In forty-five minutes he drafted three posts.

You do not need a screwdriver. Turn your phone fully off and put it in another room. Block every site except your writing tool. Then the 15-Minute Bleed: a timer, no backspace, just words on the page.

## Protocol 4: Ship at 80%

Your taste will always be ahead of your skill. So use two passes, then stop.

- **Pass 1, The Chainsaw:** structure and clarity. Does the hook work? Cut the fluff.
- **Pass 2, The Scalpel:** rhythm and format. Break up dense paragraphs. Fix typos.

No third read. No sleeping on it. Label it 80%.

## Protocol 5: the Emotional Firewall

Never publish on the day you create. Finished work sits in a "Ready to Publish" folder for at least 48 hours. By the time it goes live, your ego is no longer fused to it.

> **Pro tip from the app:** use the Timer in Mindful minutes for the 15-minute bleed. It counts down even if you leave the screen.

> **Result:** a draft in fifteen minutes, an edit in two passes, and a calm publish two days later.

## The bottom line

No escape while drafting. Two passes when editing. Two days before publishing.', 16, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l17', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Your worst-day baseline', 'lesson', NULL, 'You will have days when your brain feels like wet cement. The book says the danger is not a small day. It is a zero.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, The Minimum Viable Output: Establishing the Failure Baseline.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why dropping to zero is the real risk.
- How to set your Failure Baseline.
- Why success turns out to be boring.

## Zero breaks the chain

On a bad day, you look at your big goal, know you cannot hit it, and do nothing. "Dropping to zero is the most dangerous thing you can do." Zero restarts the compounding clock.

## Design for a rainy Tuesday

Plan for the worst evening, not the best morning. If your goal is a deep essay, your baseline might be one sentence posted. If your goal is a full video, your baseline might be a rough 15-second thought on your phone.

Write it now, in the book''s words:

"On my absolute worst days, when I have zero energy and zero motivation, I will commit to doing X."

Make X so easy that skipping it would be embarrassing.

## Boring is the secret

With the protocols in place, the book says, the work becomes "remarkably, profoundly boring." Pull an idea from the inbox. Fifteen minutes in the Blackout Box. A two-pass edit. Schedule it. Log off.

The ones who win "just have a higher tolerance for the mundane."

> **Pro tip from the app:** add your baseline as a small task on Daily with no other work beside it, ready for the day you wake up flat.

> **Result:** your chain survives bad days, so compounding never resets.

## The bottom line

Never zero. Decide your smallest output today, before the bad day comes.', 17, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l18', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The root of self-doubt', 'lesson', NULL, 'You have the ideas and the plan. Then a quiet voice asks, "Who am I to say this?"

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Imposter Syndrome and Self-Doubt: The Root of Creator Insecurity.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Where the book says creator self-doubt comes from.
- What the internet actually asks of you.
- Why you need a factory, not an art studio.

## From consumer to creator

For your whole life you have been a consumer. Consumers are safe. To create, you have to give yourself permission. That scares you, so your brain makes a feeling: being a fraud.

The book calls imposter syndrome "an ego protection mechanism." It keeps you small so you never fall.

## One question

You think others have a licence to speak. The book says the internet does not care about your background. It asks one thing: "Can you help me?" If you can solve a problem one step ahead of someone, you have something to say.

## Visibility cures the fear

The paradox: the only cure for the fear of being seen is being seen. So, the book says, treat content like a factory. "In an art studio, you wait for the muse. In a factory, you hit the quota."

And remember who is watching. They are scanning, looking for a quick fix or distraction. "You are not the center of their universe." That is freeing.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write one line on what you know that someone a step behind you does not. That is your licence.

> **Result:** you can name the fear for what it is and still publish.

## The bottom line

The doubt is protection, not truth. Help one person a step behind you.', 18, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l19', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The comparison loop', 'lesson', NULL, 'You sit down to create. It feels hard. You open the app "for a minute." You see someone''s best work. You close your draft. The book maps every step.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Diagnosis: The Comparison Loop Trap.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The five steps of the comparison loop.
- Why a swipe file can be self-doubt in disguise.
- The level 50 boss fight.

## The five steps

1. **The discomfort of creation.** Making things is hard.
2. **Relief in consumption.** You open the feed to escape.
3. **A curated masterpiece.** The feed shows you someone''s best piece.
4. **Comparing your baseline to their peak.** Your rough draft against their finished work.
5. **You rationalise your retreat.** You decide you are not ready.

## You see the Mona Lisa

You never see the sketches that were torn up. You see the finished painting. The book says that is the trick of the feed: you compare your daily reality with someone else''s polished output.

## The swipe file in disguise

"It is your imposter syndrome wearing a disguise." Studying hundreds of great hooks raises the bar until you cannot start.

## Level 1 stats

You want the level 50 boss fight right away. With level 1 stats you get crushed. The gap is often simple. "They have just been doing it longer than you."

> **Pro tip from the app:** the next time you open the feed mid-task, close it and write down which of the five steps you were on.

> **Result:** you catch the loop at step two, before it reaches step five.

## The bottom line

Do not compare your draft to their peak. Close the feed and finish the rep.', 19, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l20', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Firewall and input metrics', 'lesson', NULL, 'Willpower runs out. The book wants systems that make self-doubt beside the point.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Correction: Installations 1 and 2.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Consumption Firewall.
- Why you track inputs, not outputs.
- The book''s own story of a fourteen-email launch.

## Installation 1: the Consumption Firewall

You cannot create in the same place you consume. "Creation is output. Consumption is input. Never let the two states touch."

The rule is "Create Before Consume." When you sit down to work, the feed does not exist. Phone in another room. Apps blocked. If you must scroll, do it after you publish. Then someone else''s hit feels like data, not a threat.

## Installation 2: input metrics

When you judge yourself by likes, you let a slot machine set your worth. An input metric is fully in your control.

The book''s examples: "Write 100 words a day. Record one 60-second video. Publish one single thought." Small on purpose.

## The fourteen emails

The book''s author froze before a big launch email. So he split it into fourteen short emails, each one lesson. A sheet with fourteen boxes. One goal: send before noon. No looking at opens or sales. By day five, the fear broke.

> **Pro tip from the app:** set one small input as a repeating task on Daily. Tick it on the days you do it, whatever the numbers say.

> **Result:** you win every day the input happens, no matter what the post does.

## The bottom line

Make before you scroll. Measure what you do, not what you get.', 20, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l21', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Micro-commitments and the vault', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your brain forgets your wins and remembers every flop. The book gives you two tools to fix that.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Correction: Installations 3 and 4, and The Volume Principle.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Micro-Commitment Engine.
- The Evidence Vault.
- The Volume Principle.

## Installation 3: micro-commitments

A huge goal gives your inner critic a thousand chances to stop you. A micro-commitment is so small your brain does not see it as a threat. The book says to keep it under ten minutes and do it before you check your email.

If you commit to one sentence and write one sentence, you succeeded. You may stop. You rarely will. "The permission to stop is what removes the pressure."

## Installation 4: the Evidence Vault

A place, on paper or on screen, where you collect proof of your own action. Every publish, logged. Every finished micro-commitment, logged. Every kind comment, saved. Every thank-you from a client, stored.

When the voice says "you have no right to be here," you do not argue. You open the vault, and "let the data do the talking."

## The Volume Principle

When you post once a month, every post is a bet on your identity. When you post often, no single post matters. "Reps in a gym." The book says to act like a guide who knows the next step, not a guru who knows everything.

> **Pro tip from the app:** start your Evidence Vault today with three entries from the past month. Add one every day you publish.

> **Result:** a growing record that answers the doubt with facts.

## The bottom line

Start small, log every rep, and let the record speak.', 21, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l22', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The cost of isolation', 'lesson', NULL, 'You think you are playing a content game. The book says you are playing a relationship game.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Creator Relationships and Social Capital: The High Cost of Isolation.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why going solo puts a ceiling on you.
- How ideas actually spread.
- Why your peers matter more than giants.

## The machine has no loyalty

The platform can change its rules tomorrow and wipe out half your reach. If all you have is an audience and no relationships, you have no safety net.

The book''s author tells of going into "monk mode." No replies, no peers. The dopamine dried up and there was nobody to catch him.

## How distribution really works

"Not by shouting the loudest. But by having the right people whisper your name in rooms you are not in."

- **Ten peers at your level:** they share, mention and defend your work.
- **Serendipity:** the random message that changes everything only reaches people who are connected.
- **The bottom is a gift:** you can bond over zero engagement, test ideas together and grow up side by side.

## A hard question

"Introversion is not an excuse for invisibility." The book asks: why are you so set on doing this alone? What are you trying to prove?

> **Pro tip from the app:** list five creators at about your stage whose work you respect. Do nothing yet. Just find them.

> **Result:** you see your network, or the lack of one, as part of your growth plan.

## The bottom line

Nobody grows alone for long. Build relationships, not only an audience.', 22, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l23', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Transactional or relational', 'lesson', NULL, 'You comment on big accounts. You send a friendly message with your link. Nothing comes back. The book shows you why.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Diagnosis: Transactional vs. Relational.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The six transactional symptoms.
- The Mirror Check.
- Why social capital is measured in years.

## The six symptoms

- **The Status Filter:** you value the audience someone has, not the person.
- **The Billboard Commenter:** "This is absolute fire! So much value here!" on a big post, so their audience sees you.
- **The Trojan Horse DM:** a compliment, then "Could you take a look at my profile and give me some feedback?"
- **The Silent Scoreboard:** counting favours, then feeling cheated.
- **The "Bumping" Mentality:** a fire emoji or a "hope you''re doing well," sent only to stay top-of-mind.
- **The Expiration Date:** dropping people when they stop being useful.

## The Mirror Check

The book''s test: "Would I send this if they had zero followers?" If not, the message is about their audience, not them.

## Years, not days

"Social capital is measured in years. Not days." The book''s author admits he once was "the guy at the bar asking for fifty dollars." A stranger, a compliment, then a request.

You cannot fake caring, the book says. You can fake a hook or an edit. Not this.

> **Pro tip from the app:** read your last five messages to other creators. Run the Mirror Check on each one.

> **Result:** you can tell a message that builds trust from one that spends it.

## The bottom line

Give before you ask, and care about the person, not their numbers.', 23, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l24', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Build sideways first', 'lesson', NULL, 'Stop staring at the top of the mountain. The book says your network starts beside you.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Correction: Phases 1 to 3.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Horizontal Build and "The Grid."
- Unscalable Contribution.
- The four-part Zero-Ask message.

## Phase 1: the Horizontal Build

If you have 500 followers, someone with 50,000 is not your peer. Make a private list called "The Grid." Ten creators at your stage. Check three things:

1. **Trajectory match:** they take it seriously and post often.
2. **Value match:** you truly respect their work.
3. **Audience overlap:** the same people, from a different angle.

## Phase 2: Unscalable Contribution

Anything you can copy and paste has no value. Leave comments that add something: expand one point, share how you used it. When they launch something, share it with a paragraph on why your audience should read it. Do this for a month. Expect nothing.

## Phase 3: the Private Bridge

The first message has zero asks. No "let''s connect," no "pick your brain."

1. **The Context Anchor:** why you are here, such as their last three newsletters.
2. **The Specific Observation:** one point that changed how you work.
3. **The Unprompted Gift:** something useful they did not ask for.
4. **The Clean Exit:** no pressure to reply.

> **Pro tip from the app:** turn your five names from the last lesson into The Grid, and leave one real comment on each this week.

> **Result:** you become a familiar, useful name to your peers before you ever ask for anything.

## The bottom line

Build sideways. Contribute in ways that cannot scale. Send messages with no ask.', 24, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l25', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Your pod, giants and context', 'lesson', NULL, 'One relationship is a connection. A few, the book says, is an ecosystem.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Correction: Phases 4 to 6, and the Divergence Plateau.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three rules of a pod.
- The Case Study Strategy for reaching giants.
- The Context Engine.

## Phase 4: the pod

Invite three peers to a small group chat. No self-promotion. Share data, review each other''s hooks. The book''s author says such a group "saved my creator journey."

1. **Radical Transparency:** share failures too, or it becomes a flex group.
2. **Asynchronous Value:** voice notes and screenshots, no pressure to reply at once.
3. **Protection:** when someone launches, you all show up for it.

## Phase 5: interacting with giants

Only now look up. Do not send a giant a template. Use their idea, then send them the proof: what you did, three numbers before and after, and "I don''t need anything from you."

## Phase 6: the Context Engine

Not a spreadsheet that reminds you to text every 14 days. The book calls that "sociopathic." Just note the human details. They are moving next month. Their dog is sick. They are scared of their first launch. Remembering is "the currency of deep networks."

## When a peer takes off

The book calls it the Divergence Plateau. When a peer blows up, cheer the loudest and learn from them.

> **Pro tip from the app:** after each real conversation, add a two-line note about the person, not the business.

> **Result:** a small circle that grows with you and survives any platform change.

## The bottom line

A pod for honesty, proof for giants, and memory for everyone.', 25, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l26', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The blending-in paradox', 'lesson', NULL, 'You do everything right, and nobody notices. The book says that is exactly why.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, Attention, Positioning and Growth Mistakes: The Blending In Paradox.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why "better" loses to "different."
- Why generic value is a weak business.
- Who you are really optimising for.

## The minimum is not a strategy

Fast edits and animated captions once stood out. Today they are the minimum. Using the same hooks as everyone, you are "a green leaf" in a forest of green leaves.

People scroll fast. "They are not looking for better. They are looking for different."

## Generic value

If your audience can get the same information from ten other creators, why stay with you? The book says a script anyone could read is a business built on sand.

- **Platitudes are not positions:** "I want to help people be more productive." says nothing.
- **Speaking to everyone reaches no one.**
- **Copying big creators fails:** they have years of compounding you cannot copy.

## The machine or the human

The book says creators blend in because they optimise for the platform, not the person. Give the platform a sharp signal and it can match you with a sharp audience.

> **Pro tip from the app:** describe your work in one sentence. If ten other creators could say the same sentence, mark it "green leaf."

> **Result:** you can tell whether you are better at the same thing or truly different.

## The bottom line

Stop trying to be better at what everyone does. Be the one who does it differently.', 26, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l27', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Trends or categories', 'lesson', NULL, 'You ride a trend, get a spike, then sell almost nothing. The book explains the gap between attention and buyers.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, Diagnosis: Chasing Trends vs. Owning Categories.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three symptoms of trend-chasing.
- What owning a category means.
- What to expect when you stop chasing.

## Three symptoms

- **The Follower-to-Buyer Disconnect:** people watch, but they never act. "You have an audience, but you have no economy."
- **The Anxiety of Relevance:** a treadmill that keeps speeding up as trends get shorter.
- **The Blurry Profile:** scroll your last thirty posts. Can a stranger say what you stand for?

## Own a category

Category design, the book says, is not only for big tech companies. Do not be a "productivity guru." Be, in the book''s example, the "ADHD workflow architect for creative freelancers." A category of one.

## The cost of the switch

When you stop chasing trends, views may drop and the urge to relapse will be strong. The book says to hold. Over time, the platform starts finding your exact people for you, and they begin to search for you by name.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write a five-word category that only you could own. Test it on a friend: do they know who it is for?

> **Result:** you know the difference between a spike and a following.

## The bottom line

Trends rent you attention. A category lets you own it.', 27, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l28', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Thesis, enemy, your own words', 'lesson', NULL, '"Niche down," everyone says. The book says niching by topic is a trap. Build a worldview instead.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, Correction: the four positioning frameworks.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The Core Thesis Statement.
- The Engineered Enemy.
- Proprietary Language and the Repetition Engine.

## Framework 1: the Core Thesis

A topic like "Excel shortcuts" boxes you in. A worldview lets you talk about anything through one lens. A weak thesis: "I want to inspire entrepreneurs." Nobody can disagree. A strong one has friction.

The book''s method: write the biggest lie your industry tells. Write the opposite. That is your thesis.

## Framework 2: the Engineered Enemy

Never attack people. "Your enemy must be conceptual." A health creator''s enemy is not a rival influencer. It is the diet industry that profits from yo-yo weight. The book tells of a designer who picked an enemy, lost some fans and got fully booked by founders.

## Framework 3: Proprietary Language

Name your ideas. Use the Intersection Method: join a word from your field with one from a far-off field. The book''s examples: "Friction Loop," "Attention Architecture." When people use your words, they spread your category.

## Framework 4: the Repetition Engine

You get bored of your message long before your audience gets it. "The hook is the variable. The thesis is the constant."

> **Pro tip from the app:** write your industry''s biggest lie on one line and your thesis under it. Keep it at the top of your notes.

> **Result:** one sentence you can defend for years, and endless ways to prove it.

## The bottom line

Pick a lie to fight, name your ideas, and repeat your thesis in new ways.', 28, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l29', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The six-step plan', 'lesson', NULL, '"Theory without execution is just intellectual entertainment." So the book ends the chapter with six steps.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, The Implementation Protocol.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The six steps in order.
- How twenty angles become a month of content.
- What the purge is, and why to accept it.

## The six steps

1. **Stop posting for forty-eight hours.** "You cannot redesign the engine while the car is driving down the highway."
2. **Define the Enemy.** Write: "I stand directly against [Insert Status Quo]."
3. **Define the Thesis.** The single sentence you will defend for the next five years.
4. **Invent your Language.** Take your top three ideas. Strip the jargon. Give them names that are yours.
5. **Build the Matrix.** Twenty angles that prove your thesis: stories, metaphors, observations. "This is your content calendar for the next month."
6. **Accept the Purge.** Views may drop. People who followed you for generic content will leave. Let them.

## Why it works

The book says invisible creators stay invisible because "they refuse to make a choice." Raise a clear flag, and the right people rally to it.

> **Pro tip from the app:** do steps 2 to 5 in one sitting, then put your twenty angles on Plan my week, a few each week.

> **Result:** a clear position and a month of ideas, ready before you post again.

## The bottom line

Pause, choose your enemy and thesis, name your ideas, plan twenty angles, and let the wrong followers go.', 29, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l30', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Reach without a system', 'lesson', NULL, 'The numbers go up. You feel good. But you are popular and broke. The book says attention is not the product.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Monetization and Leverage Mistakes: Attention Without Architecture and the Broke Influencer Syndrome.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why attention is raw material.
- The four symptoms of the Broke Influencer.
- Why low prices can hurt your audience.

## Ore without a refinery

"Attention is raw material. Nothing more." Views are a pile of ore. Without a refinery, a system behind your content, you just have a heavy pile of rocks. You are "building a house on rented land" and forgot the doors.

## The four symptoms

- **The "Buy Me A Coffee" Mentality:** you play your song and leave a hat on the ground.
- **The Apologetic Pitch:** selling as if you are sorry for it.
- **The Bad Sponsorship Trap:** with nothing of your own to sell, you sell your audience to whoever pays.
- **Underpricing as a Strategy:** a cheap product protects your ego if it fails.

## Price and commitment

The book says underpricing robs your audience of the commitment needed to get a result. "You are not being generous." And a high price needs strong positioning, which is why the last chapter matters.

> **Pro tip from the app:** list everything that earns you money today. If it all depends on a platform or a sponsor, mark it "rented."

> **Result:** you can see whether you have a business or only an audience.

## The bottom line

Views are ore. Build the refinery behind them.', 30, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l31', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Capture and conversion', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your audience stops for three seconds, then swipes away. The book''s first two pillars give them a door to walk through.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Correction: Pillar One, The Capture Mechanism, and Pillar Two, The Conversion Sequence.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What a good lead magnet is.
- Why the email list is yours.
- The five-email sequence.

## Pillar One: capture

A lead magnet is a free thing that solves one problem fast. Not a ten-page ebook of loose thoughts. A template, a checklist, a five-minute video. High value, and quick to use on a phone.

When someone gives you their email, the book says, "you now own the asset." The platform can change. You still hold the keys.

## Pillar Two: the sequence

The book lays out five emails.

1. **The Delivery and The Promise:** send the free thing at once, and say what is coming.
2. **The Belief Shift:** a day later, challenge a false belief. Sell nothing.
3. **The Origin Story:** how you struggled with the same problem.
4. **The Hidden Problem and The Solution:** the deeper problem, and your paid product as the logical fix.
5. **The Logical Close:** answer common questions, explain your guarantee, give one clear call to action.

The book''s author describes waking up to two sales from strangers who had found the free checklist overnight.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write only email two this week, the belief shift. It is the one that does the most work.

> **Result:** a simple path from a stranger''s scroll to your own list, and from there to a sale.

## The bottom line

Give one useful thing for an email. Then let five honest emails do the selling.', 31, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-l32', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'The Value Ladder and evergreen', 'lesson', NULL, 'One product is not enough, and a high price on day one rarely works. The book''s third pillar is a ladder.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Correction: Pillar Three, The Architecture of Offers, and The Shift from Launching to Evergreen.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three tiers of the Value Ladder.
- Why launches burn you out.
- What a creator-founder posts.

## The Value Ladder

The book gives its prices in US dollars. These are the book''s examples, not prices for India.

- **Tier 1, the Activation Offer:** about 20 to 50 dollars. It turns a follower into a customer.
- **Tier 2, the Core Offer:** about 100 to 500 dollars. A course or manual, "the organized version of your brain."
- **Tier 3, the Premium Offer:** about 1,000 to 5,000 dollars. Your time: a cohort, a mastermind, one-to-one work. "If it requires your time, it must hurt a little bit to buy."

## From launching to evergreen

Launches: open the cart, a dozen frantic emails, a spike, then zero. The book says they burn you and your audience out. Evergreen means the system is always open, so your content just points to it.

## The creator-founder

The Broke Influencer posts "Go buy my course!" The Creator-Founder posts something of real value that naturally leads to the system. "Your only job is to be creative."

> **Pro tip from the app:** if you sell in India, set each tier in rupees for your own market. In the app''s Shop you can price in rupees and keep a product as a draft until it is ready.

> **Result:** a ladder from free to premium, and content that points to it every day.

## The bottom line

Climb with your customer one step at a time, and let the system stay open.', 32, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO course_assignments (id, program_id, title, instructions, rubric, due_days, sequence) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-creators-a01', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Your four-loop diagnosis and a two-week loop', 'Part one. Answer the book''s four Diagnostic Matrix loops (identity, communication, consistency, relationships) in a few honest lines each, and name the loop with the most yeses.

Part two. For two weeks, run the recursive loop: write, edit and publish on three different days; run every piece through the three-question Output Filter; and keep a weekly Resonance Log of your best and worst piece with the reasons.

Submit the diagnosis, links to what you published, and your two Resonance Log entries.', 'The diagnosis names one loop and gives a concrete reason. Every published piece was written and published on different days. Each Resonance Log entry names a feeling or phrase that worked and a filter question the weaker piece failed. The second week shows a change made because of the first entry.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO cohorts (id, program_id, name, start_at, end_at, capacity, status, manager_user_id, created_at) VALUES ('coh-book-dom-creators-open', 'prog-book-dom-creators', 'Self-paced, open all year', '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z', '2027-12-31T00:00:00.000Z', 1000, 'running', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');

-- Dictionary of Mistakes for 20s
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO programs (id, name, description, price_points, price_cents, currency, delivery_mode, published, outcomes, prerequisites, created_by, created_at) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s', 'Dictionary of Mistakes for 20s', 'A course from Shubham Aggarwal''s book Dictionary of Mistakes for 20s: A Field Guide to Fixing the Invisible Patterns Ruining Your 20s. Twenty-nine lessons through the eight chapters: awareness, health, money, connections, career, creation, identity and the Anti-Mistake OS with its Weekly Audit. Every lesson says which chapter it comes from; the pro tips and the notes from the app are the app''s own.', 0, 0, 'INR', 'online', 1, 'You can spot your default patterns, add friction to the bad ones, plug your money leaks, test your circle with the 3 AM Test, bring solutions instead of problems, keep an Inner Scorecard, and run a Weekly Audit that ends in three fixes.', 'None. Written for people in their twenties, useful at any age.', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l01', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Pattern blindness and your defaults', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most people do not ruin their life in one big decision. The book says they ruin it quietly, in small ways, repeated daily.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, The Invisible Mistake Loop: Why Awareness Isn''t Enough.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the book means by a default.
- The eight defaults it names.
- Why "fine" is a warning sign.

## The script runs you

Once a pattern is set, you stop deciding. "You don''t decide to hit snooze. The script hits snooze." The book calls not seeing this pattern blindness.

## Eight defaults

The book writes them like dictionary entries.

- **The Illusion of Motion:** "confusing studying with learning."
- **The Reliability Trap:** being too good at the work nobody else wants.
- **The Algorithm Leash:** letting invisible code choose your voice.
- **The Competence Chokehold:** not letting others fail, so the whole team stalls.
- **The Ledger System:** quietly tracking small grievances "to use as future ammunition."
- **The Chaos Magnet:** believing a relationship without anxiety is boring.
- **The Empathy Performance:** posting awareness instead of acting.
- **The Future-Self Deception:** believing tomorrow''s you will be a different person.

## "Fine"

"''Fine'' is the anesthesia of the modern world." The bank account bleeds, screen time climbs, posture slumps, and you say you are fine.

## Map the chain

The book traces one loop: stress, an app, a purchase, a short relief. The hidden result is a rule your brain learns. "Stress = Spend." Look at your life like an accountant: find the leak, without tears.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick the one default that made you wince. Write the last time it ran, step by step.

> **Result:** you can name one of your own scripts and see it before it runs.

## The bottom line

Small defaults, repeated daily, decide your decade. Name yours.', 1, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l02', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Entertainment or action', 'lesson', NULL, 'You feel a sting of recognition reading a book like this one. You highlight a line. The book asks: then what?

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Intellectual Entertainment vs. Action.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Four ways learning becomes a hiding place.
- The Action Mandate.
- The Therapy Implementation Rule.

## Four disguises

- **The Epiphany Chase:** "the addiction to the ''aha'' moment." Sunday night video, Monday snooze.
- **Weaponized Self-Awareness:** using therapy words to excuse the same behaviour. The book tells of a man who could explain his pattern perfectly and still pushed people away.
- **The Productivity Parody:** "building complex systems to manage work you are avoiding."
- **The Podcast Paralysis:** "the illusion that listening to highly successful people makes you successful."

The book''s picture: you consume 99% of the time and act 1%. "A massive head. And tiny, atrophied arms."

## The Action Mandate

"If you cannot apply it, do not learn it." If you read about time-blocking, block your time the very next morning. If you read about hard conversations, have one that week.

## The Therapy Implementation Rule

"Therapy is fifty minutes of talking. You have one hundred and sixty-seven hours left in the week." If you find a trigger on Tuesday, what boundary do you keep on Wednesday?

The book also asks you to stop chasing "interesting" ideas and start chasing effective ones. "Action produces data. Thinking produces anxiety."

> **Pro tip from the app:** for the next thing you read or watch to improve yourself, add one task to Daily that applies it within a day.

> **Result:** what you learn starts turning into what you do.

## The bottom line

Insight is not change. Apply it the next day, or stop collecting it.', 2, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l03', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Built for error, so add friction', 'lesson', NULL, 'The book says the modern world is "a perfectly engineered casino." You are not weak. The odds are set against you.

> **From the book:** Chapter 1, Modern Life: Optimized for Error.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The six traps of an environment built for error.
- Strategic Friction.
- The Algorithmic Purge, ISO rules and the Boredom Reset.

## The six traps

- **Frictionless Ruin:** nothing stands between an impulse and a bad choice.
- **The Slot Machine Effect:** feeds and dating apps run on random rewards.
- **Manufactured Urgency:** a false clock on your life.
- **The Hustle Mirage:** "the branding of exploitation as self-improvement."
- **Weaponized Outrage:** feeds prefer anger to nuance.
- **The Proximity Principle:** your behaviour ends up matching the design of your space.

"You cannot out-discipline an environment optimized for your failure."

## The fixes

- **Strategic Friction:** make the bad choice annoying. Log out of delivery apps. Delete saved cards.
- **The Algorithmic Purge:** unfollow and mute anything that triggers the Illusion of Motion or Manufactured Urgency.
- **Default ISO Rules:** before a dating app, write three behaviours a match must show. The book''s: they text back in reasonable time, plan real dates, and ask about your life.
- **The Boredom Reset:** "Once a week, sit in a room for thirty minutes. No phone." No book, no music. Let your brain scream.

> **Pro tip from the app:** add the Boredom Reset as a weekly repeating task on Daily.

> **Result:** your space starts working for you instead of against you.

## The bottom line

Do not fight the casino with willpower. Change the room.', 3, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l04', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The youth tax', 'lesson', NULL, 'You feel fine on four hours of sleep and three coffees. The book says you are not making energy. You are borrowing it.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, Health: Borrowed Energy, The Youth Tax.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The book''s picture of a biological credit card.
- The four ways it says to stop borrowing.
- The rules it gives for sleep and caffeine.

## A credit card

Your youth is a credit card, the book says. You pay the minimum to stay awake. The balance grows quietly. Then the bill arrives as fog, aches and burnout.

## Stop borrowing

1. **The Sleep Boundary.** The book calls sleep the non-negotiable base. Set an alarm for when you start winding down, not only for waking. Phone in the kitchen. A cheap clock by the bed.
2. **The Caffeine Protocol.** The book''s rule: wait ninety minutes after waking before coffee, and stop after 2 PM.
3. **Movement as Medicine, Not Punishment.** "Movement completes the stress cycle." No marathon. Daily movement, like a twenty-minute walk.
4. **The End of the Convenience Diet.** Pick three healthy meals you like and repeat them, so you stop deciding when tired.

"Stop aiming for perfection. Aim for consistency."

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app. These are the book''s habits, not medical advice. If you have a health condition, talk to a doctor before you change your sleep, caffeine or food.

> **Pro tip from the app:** start with one: the wind-down alarm. In the app, a repeating task with a time on Daily can be that alarm.

> **Result:** you stop borrowing from tomorrow''s energy to get through today.

## The bottom line

Pay down the balance. Sleep, slow the coffee, move, and keep meals simple.', 4, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l05', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Listen to your body', 'lesson', NULL, 'You live in your head and treat your body like a company car. The book says it has been talking to you for a while.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, The Bio-Feedback Fail: Missing Internal Signals.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How the book says the body whispers, then shouts.
- Five ways to rebuild the feedback loop.
- Why "information as anesthesia" is a trap.

## Whispers and screams

The book tells of a woman whose body sent small signals for two years: an eyelid twitch, a sore back. She treated each one with a quick fix. Then came a night she thought was a heart attack. Tests found her heart was fine; the book describes a full-body panic attack.

## Information as anesthesia

Reading about sleep is not sleeping. "You cannot out-read sleep deprivation."

## Rebuild the loop

1. **Tech-Free Buffer Zones.** No screens in the first and last thirty minutes of the day. Check in: tight shoulders? heavy stomach?
2. **The Commute to Nowhere.** A ten-minute walk before and after work, even from home.
3. **Postural Diagnostics.** Where are your shoulders? Your jaw? Your breath?
4. **The Pain Protocol.** "Pain is data." Do not just silence it.
5. **De-Intellectualizing Your Body.** Simple questions. Did I move? Have I seen the sun today?

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app. Chest pain or a scary symptom needs a doctor, not a lesson. Get checked first.

> **Pro tip from the app:** try the first and last thirty minutes screen-free for three days, and note one thing your body told you.

> **Result:** you notice the whispers before they turn into shouts.

## The bottom line

Your body sends data. Make space to hear it.', 5, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l06', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Audit your environment', 'lesson', NULL, 'You do not rise to your goals, the book says. You fall to the design of your room.

> **From the book:** Chapter 2, The Environment Audit.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why friction decides what you do.
- The five spaces to audit.
- The 15-Minute Daily Reset.

## Friction decides

Ordering food takes three taps. Cooking takes a trip to the shop. So you order. The book tells of a man who could not sleep until he moved his phone charger out of the bedroom. To scroll, he would have to get up. He fell asleep instead.

## Five spaces

1. **The Kitchen.** No chips in the cupboard. A fruit bowl in the middle. A big water bottle by your laptop.
2. **The Sleep Sanctuary.** Phone out. A cool room. The bed is for sleep.
3. **The Workspace.** A chair that holds your spine. Screen at eye level. Put things across the room so you stand up.
4. **The Digital Space.** Greyscale, an empty home screen, notifications silenced. Bury the slot machines in a folder.
5. **The Social Space.** If your circle brags about no sleep, find a "Vitality Tribe" that moves and rests well.

Start with one room, the book says. The bedroom first, for two weeks.

## The 15-Minute Daily Reset

At the end of the workday: clear the desk, put the charger back in the kitchen, and "set the water glass out for tomorrow morning." The book calls it "self-compassion translated into architecture."

> **Pro tip from the app:** add the 15-Minute Daily Reset as a repeating task on Daily with a time, at the end of your workday.

> **Result:** tomorrow''s tired you finds the room already set up to help.

## The bottom line

Stop blaming your character. Fix the room, one space at a time.', 6, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l07', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Consumer delusion', 'lesson', NULL, 'You got a raise. Six months later you are more broke than before. The book has a name for that.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Wealth: Consumer Delusion.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What consumer delusion is.
- The book''s four steps to put the pain back in paying.
- How the book defines real wealth.

## Your worth is not your cart

The book defines consumer delusion as "the subconscious belief that your worth scales with your purchases." It tells of Rohan, who got promoted and upgraded everything on EMI: the car, the flat, the drinks for the table. Six months later he looked exhausted. "Consumerism is just insecurity with a price tag."

## Four steps

1. **The De-linking Protocol.** Delete saved cards and UPI mandates from every shopping and food app. Make yourself fetch your wallet.
2. **The 48-Hour Quarantine.** Never buy anything non-essential the day you discover it. The book says most urges are gone in two days.
3. **Cost per Use.** A Rs. 2000 shirt worn once costs Rs. 2000 per use. Good shoes worn daily for a year cost about Rs. 13.
4. **Hide Your Own Money.** Move savings out the day your salary lands. "Live on the 80%. Let the 20% build your wall."

## Real wealth

"Wealth is not the ability to buy anything you want. Wealth is the ability to refuse anything you don''t want." A six-month runway. Saying no to a bad client. "An empty calendar is a higher flex than a designer watch."

> **Pro tip from the app:** do step 1 today. It takes ten minutes and makes every other step easier.

> **Result:** buying takes a moment of thought again, and savings happen before you can spend them.

## The bottom line

You are not what you buy. Put friction back, and pay yourself first.', 7, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l08', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Emotional spending', 'lesson', NULL, 'You know the maths. You still spend. The book says that is because spending is an emotional event.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Behavioral Finance vs. Academic Knowledge.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The five emotional spenders.
- The book''s five rules.
- The "hourly wage" trick.

## Knowing is not doing

The book tells of Kabir, who knew money "at a cellular level" and still ran up Rs. 8 lakhs of debt. A watch, rounds at the bar, weekend flights to Goa. "Kabir was buying anesthetics." A budget is logic. Spending, the book says, is emotion.

## Five spenders

- **Revenge:** late-night buys after a day with no control.
- **Algorithmic:** ads that find the hole and sell you the fill.
- **Frictionless:** small instalments that hide a big number.
- **Mental Accounting:** treating bonuses and refunds as "free money."
- **Sunk Cost:** spending more to justify what you already spent.

## Five rules

1. **Automate the Good, Add Friction to the Bad.** An automatic transfer to savings, for example "on the 2nd of the month."
2. **Name Your Voids.** Next to each purchase, write the feeling you had just before. A pattern shows within a week.
3. **The "Hourly Wage" Conversion.** A Rs. 15,000 shoe at your rate might be 50 hours of your life.
4. **The 24-Hour Cart Rule.** Leave it in the cart overnight. Delaying takes no willpower.
5. **Define Your "Enough."** Once you know your enough, the upsells stop working.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in Finance, add a short note to each spend this week with the feeling you had. Read them back on Sunday.

> **Result:** you can see which feeling drives your spending, and plan around it.

## The bottom line

Spending follows feelings. Name the feeling, delay the buy, and automate the saving.', 8, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l09', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Micro-leaks and the Cash Diet', 'lesson', NULL, 'A Rs. 150 charge does not hurt. The book shows what thirty of them do.

> **From the book:** Chapter 3, Compound Errors: The High Cost of Micro-Leaks.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The five kinds of micro-leak.
- The five phases to plug them.
- Why a small sum in your 20s is a big sum later.

## The maths

The book tells of a woman whose small extras, like delivery fees, surge charges and treats, added up to almost Rs. 9,700 a month. Over three years, Rs. 3,49,200. "On absolute, forgettable nothingness."

## Five leaks

- **The Convenience Tax:** fees to save a short walk.
- **Phantom Subscriptions:** "a monthly tax on the person you wish you were."
- **The "Little Treat" Delusion:** Rs. 400 a day is about Rs. 1.4 lakh a year.
- **The Up-sell Trap:** the warranty and add-on at your weakest moment.
- **Social Inflation:** keeping up with friends'' spending.

## Five phases

1. **The Bloody Audit.** Two months of statements. Highlight every non-essential spend under Rs. 1000. Add them up.
2. **The Subscription Purge.** Cancel them all. Buy back only what you truly miss.
3. **The 7-Day Convenience Fast.** Delete the quick-delivery and food apps for a week.
4. **The Cash Diet.** "Go to an ATM on Monday morning." Take out your weekly spending money, say Rs. 3000, in cash.
5. **The Opportunity Cost Multiplier.** The book''s example: Rs. 1000 spent now could be Rs. 8000 at 45.

> **Pro tip from the app:** do the Bloody Audit in Finance: look at the last two months of money out and mark each small spend that was not rent, bills or medical.

> **Result:** you find the money you did not know you were losing.

## The bottom line

Small leaks sink big ships. Find them, plug them, and let the savings grow.', 9, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l10', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Dating the same ghost', 'lesson', NULL, 'New name, same story. The book says your heart is not broken. Your selection system is.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Connections: The Prototype Error, Dating the Same Ghost.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why instant attraction can be a warning.
- The Three-Strike Micro-Behavior System.
- The book''s four rules.

## The pull

We chase the butterflies. The book says that instant, overwhelming pull is often a familiar anxiety, not compatibility. "They weren''t addicted to the person. They were addicted to the anxiety."

## Four rules

1. **Distrust the instant pull.** When you feel an obsessive rush, treat it as a yellow light. "That is not your soulmate. That is your pattern."
2. **The Three-Strike Micro-Behavior System.** Strike one: they flake with no new plan. Strike two: they leave you on read for a day while watching your stories. Strike three: they leave you confused about where you stand. Three strikes, and you step away.
3. **Stop Grading on Potential.** Do not date who they could be if they changed. "You are not a rehabilitation center."
4. **Replacement Behaviors.** When the chaos goes, you will feel empty and bored. Have something ready to fill that time, so you do not text them.

## The question to ask

When someone pulls away, do you lean in harder? The book says if you do, "your system rewards bad behavior."

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app. If someone scares you or hurts you, step away and ask for help. No rule here is worth your safety.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write your own three strikes before you meet anyone new, so the rule is set before the feelings arrive.

> **Result:** you choose by behaviour, not by the rush.

## The bottom line

The rush is not proof. Consistency is. Let the strikes decide.', 10, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l11', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The scorekeeping trap', 'lesson', NULL, 'You did the dishes three times this week. They did them once. You did not say it. You wrote it down in your head.

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Transactional Intimacy: The Scorekeeping Trap.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What a covert contract is.
- Why over-giving can be a quiet form of control.
- The book''s four rules.

## The covert contract

A covert contract, the book explains, is an agreement you made with someone, except you never told them. One of the book''s examples: "I will clean the apartment, so they will surprise me with dinner." When they do not, you resent them.

## The martyr

The book''s author admits he once did everything for everyone, building "a mountain of social debt." When he needed help, he found out. "My generosity was a manipulation tactic." Martyrs are tiring to be around.

## Four rules

1. **Give Freely or Do Not Give at All.** Before a favour, ask: if they never say thanks, would I still do this? If not, say no. "It is infinitely better to be slightly unhelpful than to be secretly resentful."
2. **Communicate Needs, Not Math.** Not "I planned the last three dates." Say: "I would love it if you planned something for us this weekend."
3. **Forgive the Asymmetry.** Some weeks you carry them. Some weeks they carry you.
4. **The Immediate Audit.** Catch the thought "I just did X, I hope they do Y." Say out loud: "I chose to do X. They do not owe me anything."

> **Pro tip from the app:** this week, turn one complaint into a plain request using the book''s sentence shape.

> **Result:** people start showing up for you because they no longer feel managed.

## The bottom line

Burn the ledger. Ask for what you need, and give only what you give freely.', 11, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l12', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The 3 AM Test', 'lesson', NULL, 'Thousands of followers. Hundreds of contacts. The book asks: who would come if you were stuck at 3 AM?

> **From the book:** Chapter 4, Social Decay: Low-Resolution Networking.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Utility networks and resilience networks.
- The 3 AM Test.
- Positive friction and awkward silence.

## Applause or a net

The book''s author once launched a project to five hundred contacts. When it failed, he could not call any of them. "You are collecting people who can applaud you. You are not collecting people who can catch you."

A utility network is useful. A resilience network is "the three to five people who keep your roof from caving in."

## Five rules

1. **Kill the Broadcast.** Share big news with the few first, not the feed.
2. **Say no honestly.** "I''m so glad to see you doing well, but my schedule is entirely maxed out right now."
3. **The 3 AM Test.** Write your closest names. Stranded at 3 AM, tyre blown, no money, raining. Who wakes up and drives an hour? Cross off the rest. If you end at zero, "that is the baseline."
4. **Create Positive Friction.** "Work on a difficult project together. Travel somewhere uncomfortable together." Smooth friendships have no grip.
5. **Embrace the Awkward Silence.** Real friends can sit quietly together.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in Bond, the app''s friends area, the people you would call at 3 AM are the ones to keep close.

> **Result:** you know who is in your real circle, and you invest there.

## The bottom line

Fewer, deeper people. Test your circle, then build it with shared hard things.', 12, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l13', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Stop waiting for permission', 'lesson', NULL, 'School gave you a syllabus and a rubric. Work does not. The book says that is the first trap of a career.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Career: The Passive Professional, The Myth of Meritocracy.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why "doing good work" is not enough.
- The four corrections.
- How to report a problem the useful way.

## The myth

You think merit means skill, hours and no typos. The book tells of Aman, a gifted designer who quietly did every brief. A designer half as good was promoted, because that one pushed back and offered better ideas.

## Four corrections

1. **The 80/20 Rule of Permission.** For the 80% of low-cost choices, move from "Can I do X?" to "I am doing X unless you tell me otherwise." Ask only on the 20% that matter.
2. **Bring Solutions, Not Problems.** Attach the context, two options and your pick. The book''s example: "The client is angry about the delay. We can either refund 10% of the fee, or we can add a free consulting hour next week. I recommend the consulting hour..."
3. **Solve the Unstated Problem.** The book''s examples: build a better onboarding document, or set up "a 15-minute weekly sync" between two teams that keep missing each other.
4. **Manage the Outcome, Not the Task.** A 10-page report is a task. "Clarity for the executive team" is the outcome. A 2-page dashboard might do it better.

"You stop looking at your boss as a teacher. You start looking at your boss as your first client."

> **Pro tip from the app:** next time you are about to report a problem, stop and write two options and your pick first.

> **Result:** your manager starts saying "yes" instead of solving your problems for you.

## The bottom line

Act on small things, bring answers, and own the outcome.', 13, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l14', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'From doer to leader', 'lesson', NULL, 'You were the best at the work, so they gave you a team. The book says this is where careers quietly go off a cliff.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, The Leadership Lag.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The "faster if I do it" trap.
- See One, Do One, Teach One.
- The six corrections.

## The trap

You know the answer instantly, so you fix it yourself. You redo a junior''s B-minus work. You "delegate" but ask for updates every two hours. The book tells of Vikram, a gifted coder made CTO, who rewrote his team''s work himself. Bugs piled up, the junior engineers quit, and the board let him go. "Do not let your competence become your cage."

## Six corrections

1. **Accept the 80% Rule.** Most clients cannot tell 80% from 100%.
2. **Delegate Outcomes.** Hand over the result, not the steps. "Manage the outcome, not the method."
3. **See One, Do One, Teach One.** You do it and explain your thinking. They do it while you watch, hands off. Then they write the guide, step by step, for the next person.
4. **Fire Yourself Every Six Months.** Hand off the routine bottom 20% of your tasks.
5. **Ban the "Quick Question."** Ask them to come back in ten minutes with a proposed answer.
6. **Shift the Reward System.** Take pride in what your team ships, not in being the hero.

"Heroes burn out... Architects build castles that stand long after they are gone."

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick one task you do every week. Run See One with someone this week.

> **Result:** your team grows, and you get time back for the work only you can do.

## The bottom line

Stop being the hero. Teach, hand over outcomes, and fire yourself from the routine.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l15', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Network myopia', 'lesson', NULL, '"I just keep my head down and do my work." The book says that sentence quietly caps careers.

> **From the book:** Chapter 5, Network Myopia.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why great work alone often goes unseen.
- The vertical prisoner.
- The seven corrections.

## The myth of the work speaking for itself

You save the company money, upload the report, and wait. Nobody notices. People favour those they know, like and trust. The book tells of Kabir, who only spoke to his own manager. After two years, he was passed over.

## Seven corrections

1. **Map the Real Org Chart.** Who do people actually listen to? Pitch them first.
2. **The Give-First Conversation.** Two no-agenda chats a week with someone in another team. "I''d love to grab a 15-minute virtual coffee just to learn what your team actually does."
3. **Manage Your Own PR.** Share results, not effort.
4. **Master the Pre-Meeting.** Call the biggest sceptic two days before your pitch.
5. **Build Weak Ties.** Light, real contact with people outside your circle.
6. **Mentor to Sponsor.** Find leaders who can speak for you in rooms you are not in.
7. **Embrace the Social System.** "It is just a network. Learn to see the nodes."

> **Pro tip from the app:** add the two give-first conversations as a weekly task on Daily.

> **Result:** people across your workplace know who you are and what you do well.

## The bottom line

Good work needs good relationships to be seen. Give first, and build the network on purpose.', 15, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l16', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Research that stalls', 'lesson', NULL, 'A month building the perfect content dashboard. Not one video posted. The book calls this the consumption loop.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Creation: The Consumption Loop, Research as Procrastination.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The four traps that look like preparation.
- The five systems to break them.
- The Output-First Morning.

## Four traps

- **Gear:** "Buying a better microphone just makes your bad ideas sound clearer."
- **Software:** a beautiful system for work you are avoiding.
- **Course:** you finish the modules and do nothing.
- **Strategy:** endless planning, so you never get rejected.

"Failure is data. Research is theory."

## Five systems

1. **Just-In-Time Learning.** Learn what today''s work needs, not what you might need someday.
2. **The 24-Hour Implementation Rule.** Use one idea from anything you learn within 24 hours.
3. **Forced Scarcity, the Anti-Gear Protocol.** "You earn the microphone after episode ten." Record the first episodes on your phone.
4. **The Output-First Morning.** "For the first two hours of your workday, the internet is off." Only your making tools.
5. **The "Ugly Draft" Mandate.** "Your goal for the first draft is not to make it good. Your goal is to make it exist."

> **Pro tip from the app:** block your first two hours as one task on Daily called "Make first." Tick it only when something exists that did not before.

> **Result:** you make before you consume, and your first drafts finally exist.

## The bottom line

Preparation is not progress. Make the ugly draft first.', 16, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l17', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Metric entrapment', 'lesson', NULL, 'You get your first taste of traction. Then you ask, "How do I get more views?" The book says that is the moment you lose control.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Metric Entrapment.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How numbers can rise while the business fails.
- The seven systems.
- The Burner Account Test.

## Success that is failure

The book tells of Aisha, a designer who went viral making fun of a bad client, then kept doing it. Half a million followers. Brands sent free keyboards. Podcasts invited her. She did not book one design client. "The illusion of success masking the reality of failure."

## Seven systems

1. **"Who" Over "How Many."** Three people truly moved beat 100,000 views of fire emojis.
2. **The 80/20 Rule of Platform Compliance.** Play the platform''s game enough to be seen, not so much you lose yourself.
3. **The 1-in-4 Rebellion.** Every fourth post, make something just for the people you want.
4. **The Burner Account Test.** Is it your ideas or the format that works? The book suggests testing without your name on it.
5. **Rent-to-Own Migration.** Followers on a platform are rented. Move them to something you own, like an email list.
6. **The Long-Form Anchor.** Short videos are the net. Long pieces are where your best thinking lives.
7. **The Monetization Litmus Test.** Offer something paid, to test real trust.

> **Pro tip from the app:** for your last ten posts, write down how many people replied with a real question. That is your "who" number.

> **Result:** you measure the people you reach, not only how many.

## The bottom line

Views are rented. Move people to what you own, and count who you reach.', 17, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l18', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The solopreneur ceiling', 'lesson', NULL, 'You have clients, cash and "Founder" in your bio. The book asks what happens if you get sick for a week.

> **From the book:** Chapter 6, Scaling Failures: The Solopreneur Ceiling.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why a one-person machine has a ceiling.
- The Brutal Time Audit and Trash, Transfer, Trim.
- Asynchronous Leverage.

## Your blood runs the machine

The book tells of Rohan, a solo creator who caught a bad flu and spent ten days in bed. He could not type. His business fell apart in a week. If you stop, the money stops. One trap is the Admin Avalanche: your best hours go to email and invoices.

## Seven systems

1. **The Brutal Time Audit.** Track a week in three columns. A, the Magic: only you can do it. B, the Mechanics: needed, but not your brain. C, the Waste. Remove C, hand off B, live in A.
2. **Trash, Transfer, Trim.** For every task in column B: does it need doing at all? Can someone or something else do it? Can it be smaller?
3. **Agentic Automation.** Software, AI or a trained person that runs without you.
4. **The SOP Machine.** Write the steps down, so a new helper can start the next day.
5. **The Productize Pivot.** Turn a custom service into a set product.
6. **Asynchronous Leverage.** "Real-time communication is the enemy of deep work." Ask for "one end-of-day report" instead of constant messages.
7. **The "Fire Yourself" Mandate.** Hand off admin, then editing, then more.

> **Pro tip from the app:** run the three-column audit on just one day this week. Pick one B task to trash, transfer or trim.

> **Result:** you get hours back for the work only you can do.

## The bottom line

A business that needs all of you every day is a job. Audit, hand off, and write it down.', 18, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l19', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Stuck at eighteen', 'lesson', NULL, '"I am someone who doesn''t do mornings." "I am someone who is bad with money." The book asks: who decided that, and when?

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, Identity: The Error of the Static Self, The Eighteen-Year-Old Anchor.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why old labels can hold you back.
- The five steps to lift the anchor.
- Identity as a wardrobe.

## Anchored to a ghost

The book says many people are tied to the version of themselves they were around eighteen: the smart kid, the rebel, the caretaker, the hustler. It looks like consistency. It works like a chain.

## Five steps

1. **The Identity Audit.** Write every label you use. "I am someone who..." Ask which ones still help.
2. **The Resume of Failures.** List the job you did not get, the project that flopped. Treat each as data, not proof you are broken.
3. **"Kill Your Clones."** For each old label, find its default move and do the opposite on purpose.
4. **Identity as a Wardrobe.** The book''s line: "Your values are the body. Your identity is the clothing." Keep your values. Change what you wear for the room you are in.
5. **Embrace the Void.** For a while, the old labels will not fit and the new ones will feel fake. Do not panic. Stay there.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick one "I am someone who…" line you want to retire. This week, do one thing that proves it wrong.

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app. Feeling lost for a while is normal. If the empty feeling turns dark or stays for weeks, talk to someone you trust or a professional.

> **Result:** your labels become choices, not cages.

## The bottom line

You are not stuck as who you were at eighteen. Keep your values, and change your clothes.', 19, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l20', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Your Inner Scorecard', 'lesson', NULL, 'If the numbers are high, you feel valuable. If they drop, you feel worthless. The book says you have floated your identity on the public market.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, External Validation: Outsourced Self-Worth.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The three markets of outside approval.
- The Inner Scorecard.
- Metric Isolation and the Secret Action System.

## Three markets

The digital market (likes), the professional market (praise and titles) and the relational market (being chosen). The book tells of a founder whose identity was owned by investors and the press. When the market turned, "the world can un-name you."

## Five steps

1. **The Inner Scorecard.** Write three measures fully in your control. Not a promotion, but "one hour of deep, uninterrupted work every morning." Not 100 likes, but "writing 500 words of truth every day." Grade yourself on these at night.
2. **Metric Isolation.** Post, then do not look at the numbers for 48 hours. Turn off read receipts.
3. **The Secret Action System.** Do one hard, valuable thing in total silence "for at least six months." No posts, no mentions.
4. **Decoding the Echoes.** When praise or criticism comes, read it as data, not a verdict.
5. **The Value Audit.** Every 90 days, ask: who am I trying to impress right now?

> **Pro tip from the app:** in the app''s Reviews you can set your own business metrics, but the book''s Inner Scorecard needs no app. Write your three on paper by your bed.

> **Result:** a steady sense of worth that does not swing with the numbers.

## The bottom line

Grade yourself on what you control. Let the rest be data.', 20, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l21', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Test, do not search', 'lesson', NULL, 'Go travel. Go journal. Go "find" who you truly are. The book says that is backwards. You do not find who you are. You build it.

> **From the book:** Chapter 7, Identity Testing: The High-Volume Experiment.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- The archaeologist and the architect.
- The five-step testing playbook.
- The Energy Audit question.

## Two ways to look for yourself

The archaeologist digs, sure there is a true self buried somewhere. The book tells of Rohan, who built a complex matrix of passions and called every month with a new epiphany. He never built anything. The architect throws up a rough frame, sees what works and builds on it.

## The playbook

1. **Lower the Barrier.** Do not quit your job to test a business. Start a project on a Saturday morning.
2. **Set a thirty-day limit.** Long enough to get past the awful first week, short enough to survive if you hate it. A test that ends early is still data.
3. **The Energy Audit.** Not "Am I passionate?" Ask: "Did this activity drain my energy, or did it generate energy?"
4. **Public Failure as a Filter.** Try it where people can see. If failing makes you want to try again, it may be your path.
5. **The Ruthless Pivot.** Drop what drained you without mourning it.

The book says testing never truly ends. There is no final form.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick one small test and set it as a 30-day repeating task on Daily, with an end day.

> **Result:** you learn who you are from what you did, not what you imagined.

## The bottom line

Build, do not dig. Run small tests and follow the energy.', 21, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l22', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The Weekly Audit: set it up', 'lesson', NULL, 'Most people never look back at their week. The book says your life is "the sum of your unexamined weeks."

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Anti-Mistake OS: The Weekly Audit.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What the Weekly Audit is for.
- How to set it up.
- The one rule about guilt.

## Hunting mistakes

The audit is not journaling about feelings or planning a dream life. "You are hunting for mistakes." The small errors that crept into the week, before they set into habits.

Without it, the book says, you are driving with tape over the dashboard.

## Set the stage

- **Pick a day.** "Most people choose Sunday evening. Or Sunday morning. Or Friday afternoon." "The specific day does not matter. The consistency matters."
- **Block 30 to 60 minutes.** Treat it like a meeting with your most important investor: you.
- **Phone off. Tabs closed.** Use a notebook and pen, or one clean document.

## No guilt

"Guilt is useless." It lets you feel bad without fixing anything. When you spot a mistake, the book''s reply is: "Fascinating. The system failed. Why?"

## Ask "why" until you hit the root

The book''s example: skipped the gym. Why? Tired. Why? Slept badly Tuesday. Why? Scrolling at 1 AM. Keep going until you find the cause you can change.

> **Pro tip from the app:** in Reviews, you can schedule a weekly review on the day and time you pick, and the inbox will remind you.

> **Result:** a fixed weekly slot where mistakes get caught while they are still small.

## The bottom line

Same day, same time, no guilt. Ask why until you hit the root.', 22, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l23', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Audit health and wealth', 'lesson', NULL, 'The first two domains of the audit are your body and your money. The book''s questions are blunt.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Weekly Audit: Domain 1, Health, and Domain 2, Wealth.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What to look for in your health this week.
- What to look for in your money.
- How to find the trigger under a mistake.

## Health

Look at sleep, food and movement. Where did you fall short? Do not stop at "Skipped gym." Trace it back, the book says, like the chain from a skipped workout to a late night of scrolling.

## Wealth

Open your statements. Look for:

- **Impulse buys.**
- **Leaks.** Small charges that added up.
- **System failures.** "The subscription you still haven''t canceled."

"Most financial advice is useless because behavior beats knowledge." The audit shows you the trigger. Did you spend more on Thursday? Was Thursday a hard day at work? Then you were not buying food. You were buying relief.

## Link the two

Often the same trigger hits both. A bad night leads to takeaway and a skipped walk. The book wants you to see the shared root.

> **Pro tip from the app:** the app''s Finance and its period view can show you this week''s money in and out in one place for this domain.

> **Result:** you know the one habit that hit both your body and your wallet this week.

## The bottom line

Check the body and the bank. Find the trigger under both.', 23, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l24', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Audit connections, career, creation', 'lesson', NULL, 'Next come your people and your work. The book says the patterns here hide behind other people''s names.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Weekly Audit: Domains 3 and 4, Connections, and Career and Creation.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- What to ask about your relationships.
- What to ask about your work and what you made.
- The book''s story of a vague brief.

## Connections

"Dating problems are pattern problems, not ''people problems.''" If you keep meeting the same type, the variable is your selection and what you tolerate.

Look at friendships too. Did you say yes to something you wanted to refuse? Why? Fear of missing out? Not knowing how to say no?

## Career and creation

The book''s author looked at his own work and found his briefs were vague: "Make it look cool." "Get it to me whenever this week." He was trying to be the easy-going boss, and his small habits caused the big failures.

Ask:

- **What exists now that did not exist on Monday?**
- **What rolled over all week, and why?** Too hard, or too unclear?
- **Consumption or creation?** Which did you do more of?

> **Pro tip from the app:** look at the tasks on Daily that slid from day to day this week. Those are your answer to the second question.

> **Result:** you can see which people and which work habits shaped your week.

## The bottom line

Look at who you said yes to and what you actually made. The pattern is there.', 24, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l25', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Time, attention and identity', 'lesson', NULL, 'The last two domains are the ones people like to skip. The book asks you to look at the raw numbers.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Weekly Audit: Domains 5 and 6, and the To-Don''t list.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- How to audit your time and attention.
- How to audit your identity.
- What the audit produces: a To-Don''t list.

## Time and attention

Open your screen time. "Do not look away. Let the number sting." If you spent 14 hours on social media this week, "that is a part-time job." You worked it for free.

## Identity

Where did you act out an old version of yourself out of habit? The "party guy" who goes out when he wants to sleep. The "helper" who does someone else''s work at the cost of their own.

## Connect the dots

Without the audit, you fix symptoms: a new budget, a new app. The audit "shows you the root."

## The output

Not a to-do list. A "To-Don''t" list: a few structural fixes so the mistake cannot happen again. "Do not try to fix 50 things at once. Pick the top three." Next week, audit again. The big mistakes fade, and you find smaller ones.

The book adds that thirty minutes a week is the cheapest way to save years of trouble.

> **Pro tip from the app:** the app''s not to do list on Daily is a natural home for your three To-Don''ts. Tick each day you keep away from them.

> **Result:** three clear fixes each week, built into your days.

## The bottom line

Look at the screen time and the old roles. Then pick three things not to do.', 25, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l26', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The Decisive Pivot', 'lesson', NULL, 'You found the mistake. Now what? The book says knowing without acting "is just a more sophisticated form of procrastination."

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Decisive Pivot: The Art of Fast Correction.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why grand plans fail.
- The smallest possible pivot.
- The sunk cost trap.

## Put out the fire

If your kitchen is on fire, you do not analyse the smoke. Most people over-think a mistake. The book says treat it like a fire.

## Not a grand plan

A grand plan "never survives the week." It needs a whole new you by Monday. A pivot "requires a single, structural shift in your environment." Design the smallest change that makes the mistake impossible to repeat.

## Act fast

Within minutes of seeing a mistake, your brain makes excuses. "It wasn''t that bad." "Next week will be different." The book says to act before the excuses arrive.

## Sunk cost

"You are paying for the same mistake twice. Once with your past. And again with your future." The book''s author spent three months tweaking a clever onboarding system his clients hated. He only stopped when a big client almost left.

## The maths of one bad night

Stay up late every Tuesday and Wednesday is ruined. "Fifty-two ruined days" a year. One small pivot wins them back.

> **Pro tip from the app:** for the top mistake from your last audit, write the smallest change to your room or phone that stops it. Do it today.

> **Result:** mistakes get fixed the week you find them.

## The bottom line

Small, fast, structural. Fix it now, and stop paying twice.', 26, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l27', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Pivots in every area', 'lesson', NULL, 'The book gives a ready pivot for each part of life. They are deliberately plain.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Decisive Pivot: pivots for wealth, connection and health, and the "Good Enough" Fix.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- A pivot for money, for people and for health.
- Why a good-enough fix beats a perfect one.
- How to hold a rule when the craving comes.

## Three pivots

- **Wealth:** you spent on random buys while bored. Delete saved card details. Make buying slower.
- **Connection:** you said yes to things you did not want. Use one line: "Let me check my schedule and get back to you." That buffer is the whole pivot.
- **Health:** you ate cookies every day at 4 PM. Throw them away now. Not a higher shelf. Away.

## Good enough

"A fast pivot is usually ugly." A blocked site. A blunt message. Duct tape on a leak. The book says elegance is a luxury; the fix only has to work.

## Hold the line

You will want to undo the rule midweek. "Your Sunday brain was the architect. Your Wednesday brain is just an addict looking for a hit." Change the rule at the next audit, not in the craving.

Over the months, the book says, the big mistakes vanish and your environment protects you.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write "Let me check my schedule and get back to you" in your phone''s notes so it is ready when someone asks.

> **Result:** a simple, ready fix for the most common slips in each area.

## The bottom line

Ugly fixes that work beat perfect plans that wait. Hold the rule until the next audit.', 27, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l28', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Build the system', 'lesson', NULL, 'Your own system works. The book''s last step is to build systems that protect other people too.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, Contribution: From Self-Fix to System-Build.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Why advice rarely helps.
- The architect''s mindset.
- The Work, Shared and Social OS.

## Advice is cheap

"Advice is the most useless currency in the modern world." Everyone gives it. Nobody takes it. Think how much good advice you ignored.

## Be an architect

"An advisor tells you to be careful on the stairs. An architect installs a handrail." The book''s author spent a year sending a friend money tips that changed nothing. Then he helped him set up an automatic investment. The money left before stress could spend it.

## Three systems

- **Work OS:** when someone makes a mistake, ask "Where did the system fail to protect this employee?" before you blame them.
- **Shared OS:** rules that skip the ego in a relationship. The book''s examples: "No Hard Conversations After 9 PM," a shared account for fixed bills with separate ones for personal spending, and a 24-hour pause on purchases over $200 (the book''s own figure).
- **Social OS:** your space is shaped by the people you spend most time with. Plan the healthy invitation yourself: a walk, not another round.

> **Pro tip from the app:** pick one rule from the Shared OS and suggest it to your partner or housemate this week.

> **Result:** the people around you get a handrail, not a lecture.

## The bottom line

Stop giving advice. Change the room people stand in.', 28, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO learning_items (id, program_id, module_title, content_type, content_ref, body, sequence, published) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-l29', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'The boundary of contribution', 'lesson', NULL, 'You cannot save everyone. The book closes on that hard truth, and on why the system is still worth building.

> **From the book:** Chapter 8, The Boundary of Contribution and The Dictionary Closes.

In this lesson you''ll learn:

- Where your help should stop.
- What your system teaches others.
- How the book closes.

## Some will resist

When you build better systems, you notice who adapts and who fights them. "An architect can build a perfect house. But if the tenant wants to burn it down," you cannot stop them.

The book is direct. If a partner refuses the 9 PM rule and insists on midnight shouting matches, you leave. If a friend refuses every healthy invite and only calls to complain, you stop answering. "Your Anti-Mistake OS is your fortress."

## What it teaches

A team with clear systems trains people who go on to build their own. A relationship with clear boundaries shows a partner, and later children, "what love actually looks like. Love is not chaotic passion. Love is a reliable system of mutual care."

## The close

The world, the book says, is built to break your focus and keep you tired and broke. The default path is "a slow, quiet failure." You do not have to accept the default.

> **Careful:** this is a note from the app. Leaving a relationship can be hard and sometimes unsafe. If you feel at risk, reach out to someone you trust or a local helpline before you act.

> **Pro tip from the app:** write your own one-line rule for where your help stops. Keep it beside your Weekly Audit notes.

> **Result:** you help where help is wanted, and protect your system where it is not.

## The bottom line

Build the house, open the gates, and keep the walls standing.', 29, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO course_assignments (id, program_id, title, instructions, rubric, due_days, sequence) VALUES ('prog-book-dom-20s-a01', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Four weeks of the Weekly Audit', 'Pick your audit day and block 30 to 60 minutes, as the book says. For four weeks, run the Weekly Audit over the six domains (health, wealth, connections, career and creation, time and attention, identity).

Each week, write the three structural corrections you chose (your To-Don''t list) and, the next week, whether each one held.

Submit the four weeks of notes: the three corrections each week, what held, and one pivot you made the same week you found the mistake.', 'All four audits happened on the chosen day. Each week names exactly three structural corrections, written as changes to the environment rather than promises. Each later week reports honestly on the earlier corrections. At least one pivot was made the same week the mistake was found.', 14, 1);
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO cohorts (id, program_id, name, start_at, end_at, capacity, status, manager_user_id, created_at) VALUES ('coh-book-dom-20s-open', 'prog-book-dom-20s', 'Self-paced, open all year', '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z', '2027-12-31T00:00:00.000Z', 1000, 'running', NULL, '2026-11-04T00:00:00.000Z');
